1
00:03:00,430 --> 00:03:02,724
Amazing. This is amazing!

2
00:03:02,975 --> 00:03:06,186
Listen to this:
The entire work force of Virginia...

3
00:03:06,436 --> 00:03:08,897
...had solitaire removed
from their computers...

4
00:03:09,481 --> 00:03:11,483
...because they hadn't done
any work in six weeks.

5
00:03:11,733 --> 00:03:12,568
That's so sad.

6
00:03:12,818 --> 00:03:14,486
Do you know what this is?

7
00:03:14,736 --> 00:03:17,698
The end of Western civilization
as we know it.

8
00:03:18,699 --> 00:03:19,616
Aren't you late?

9
00:03:19,950 --> 00:03:23,704
Name me one thing that
we've gained from technology.

10
00:03:23,954 --> 00:03:24,955
Electricity.

11
00:03:25,372 --> 00:03:28,917
That's one. You think this machine's
your friend, but it's not.

12
00:03:29,168 --> 00:03:31,253
- I'm out of here.
- See you tonight!

13
00:03:31,503 --> 00:03:32,838
Sushi!

14
00:03:33,797 --> 00:03:35,007
Bye!

15
00:04:24,389 --> 00:04:25,265
Welcome.

16
00:04:27,518 --> 00:04:29,478
You've got mail.

17
00:04:38,570 --> 00:04:41,281
"Brinkley is my dog.
We both love New York streets."

18
00:04:41,698 --> 00:04:44,368
Although he likes to eat pizza
off the sidewalk...

19
00:04:44,618 --> 00:04:45,577
...and I prefer to buy it.

20
00:04:46,745 --> 00:04:49,873
Brinkley is a great catcher who was
offered a tryout on the Mets.

21
00:04:50,123 --> 00:04:53,252
But he chose to stay with me
so he could spend 18 hours a day...

22
00:04:53,502 --> 00:04:56,839
...sleeping on a large pillow
the size of an inner tube.

23
00:04:57,214 --> 00:04:59,007
Don't you love New York in the fall?

24
00:04:59,258 --> 00:05:01,760
Makes me want to buy school supplies.

25
00:05:02,177 --> 00:05:03,095
I'm almost ready.

26
00:05:03,345 --> 00:05:06,557
I would send you a bouquet
of newly sharpened pencils...

27
00:05:06,807 --> 00:05:09,184
...if I knew your name and address.

28
00:05:09,434 --> 00:05:13,021
On the other hand,
this not knowing has its charms.

29
00:05:13,397 --> 00:05:14,731
Did you push it?

30
00:05:15,065 --> 00:05:16,567
Yes, I pushed it.

31
00:05:17,025 --> 00:05:18,527
I'm so late.

32
00:05:20,195 --> 00:05:22,781
Random House fired Dick Atkins.
Good riddance!

33
00:05:23,031 --> 00:05:25,951
Murray Chilton died. That's one
less person I'm not speaking to.

34
00:05:26,201 --> 00:05:27,911
Hurry! Hurry! Hurry!

35
00:05:31,498 --> 00:05:34,459
Vince got a great review.
He'll be insufferable.

36
00:05:35,002 --> 00:05:36,211
Tonight, PEN dinner.

37
00:05:37,504 --> 00:05:38,797
Am I going?

38
00:05:39,047 --> 00:05:39,882
You promised!

39
00:05:41,758 --> 00:05:42,759
It's black-tie.

40
00:05:43,719 --> 00:05:45,512
Can't I just give money instead?

41
00:05:45,762 --> 00:05:47,973
What is it this week?
Free Albanian writers?

42
00:05:48,807 --> 00:05:49,933
I'm in favor of that.

43
00:05:53,604 --> 00:05:54,980
Okay, I'll go.

44
00:05:55,272 --> 00:05:56,231
You're late.

45
00:05:56,481 --> 00:05:57,774
I know, I know.

46
00:06:31,808 --> 00:06:34,853
Who's a happy dog?

47
00:06:41,193 --> 00:06:42,152
Welcome.

48
00:06:43,987 --> 00:06:45,614
You've got mail.

49
00:06:45,864 --> 00:06:48,283
All right. Okay.
Get down, get down.

50
00:06:51,828 --> 00:06:54,540
Dear friend: I like to start
my notes to you...

51
00:06:54,790 --> 00:06:57,000
...as if we're already
in the middle of a conversation.

52
00:06:57,376 --> 00:06:59,878
I pretend that we're the oldest
and dearest friends...

53
00:07:00,128 --> 00:07:01,672
...as opposed to what
we actually are:

54
00:07:02,256 --> 00:07:04,508
People who don 't know
each other's names...

55
00:07:04,758 --> 00:07:07,803
...and met in a chat room where
we both claimed we'd never been before.

56
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:12,057
"What will NY152 say today?" I wonder.

57
00:07:13,392 --> 00:07:15,227
I turn on my computer.

58
00:07:15,477 --> 00:07:18,146
I wait impatiently as it connects.

59
00:07:18,397 --> 00:07:20,148
I go online...

60
00:07:20,399 --> 00:07:24,987
...and my breath catches in my chest
until I hear three little words:

61
00:07:25,487 --> 00:07:26,655
"You've got mail."

62
00:07:27,990 --> 00:07:31,827
I hear nothing, not even a sound
on the streets of New York.

63
00:07:32,077 --> 00:07:34,162
Just the beat of my own heart.

64
00:07:34,413 --> 00:07:36,123
I have mail...

65
00:07:36,331 --> 00:07:37,624
...from you.

66
00:08:38,810 --> 00:08:41,605
The electrical contractor
hit a deer last night.

67
00:08:41,813 --> 00:08:43,732
So he won't be here till tomorrow.

68
00:08:43,941 --> 00:08:45,859
And upstairs, the shelves are late...

69
00:08:46,068 --> 00:08:47,903
...because the pine
we ordered has beetles.

70
00:08:48,153 --> 00:08:49,029
Very good.

71
00:08:49,238 --> 00:08:52,324
And we got a $50,000 ticket
for workers peeing off the roof.

72
00:08:53,951 --> 00:08:56,370
That is great.
Is the electrician here?

73
00:08:56,620 --> 00:09:00,624
I just told you he hit a deer.
I knew you weren't listening to me.

74
00:09:01,500 --> 00:09:03,293
You're right. I wasn't.

75
00:09:03,544 --> 00:09:05,128
"I hear nothing.

76
00:09:05,504 --> 00:09:08,966
Not a sound on the city streets,
just the beat of my own heart."

77
00:09:09,174 --> 00:09:12,010
I think that's how it goes.
Something like that.

78
00:09:12,427 --> 00:09:15,514
You and Patricia got engaged,
didn't you? You can tell me.

79
00:09:15,764 --> 00:09:16,765
Engaged?

80
00:09:17,182 --> 00:09:18,141
Are you crazy?

81
00:09:18,392 --> 00:09:20,060
I thought you liked Patricia.

82
00:09:20,269 --> 00:09:21,186
I do.

83
00:09:21,436 --> 00:09:23,522
I love Patricia.

84
00:09:24,648 --> 00:09:27,776
Patricia's amazing.
She makes coffee nervous.

85
00:09:27,985 --> 00:09:31,572
We should announce ourselves to
the neighborhood: "Here we come."

86
00:09:31,989 --> 00:09:33,615
This is the Upper West Side.

87
00:09:33,866 --> 00:09:36,118
We might as well tell them
we're opening a crack house.

88
00:09:36,368 --> 00:09:37,452
They're going to hate us.

89
00:09:37,786 --> 00:09:41,540
They'll be lining up to picket
the big bad chain store...

90
00:09:41,790 --> 00:09:42,833
...that's out to destroy...

91
00:09:43,083 --> 00:09:44,209
Everything they hold dear.

92
00:09:44,459 --> 00:09:47,921
We're going to seduce them
with our square footage...

93
00:09:48,172 --> 00:09:51,842
...and our discounts
and our deep armchairs...

94
00:09:52,050 --> 00:09:53,969
...and our cappuccino.

95
00:09:54,178 --> 00:09:56,555
They're going to hate us
at the beginning...

96
00:09:56,847 --> 00:09:58,682
...but we'll get them in the end.

97
00:09:58,932 --> 00:09:59,808
And you know why?

98
00:10:00,058 --> 00:10:01,101
Why?

99
00:10:01,560 --> 00:10:04,730
Because we'll sell cheap books
and legal addictive stimulants.

100
00:10:04,938 --> 00:10:07,816
In the meantime,
we'll just put up a big sign:

101
00:10:08,066 --> 00:10:10,986
"Coming Soon, a Fox Books Superstore.
The End of Civilization...

102
00:10:11,236 --> 00:10:12,571
...As You Know It!"

103
00:10:16,909 --> 00:10:18,368
Good morning, Christina.

104
00:10:18,577 --> 00:10:19,745
Morning, Kathleen.

105
00:10:20,078 --> 00:10:23,332
Isn't it just the most beautiful day?

106
00:10:23,832 --> 00:10:25,834
I guess. Yeah, sure.

107
00:10:27,586 --> 00:10:30,464
- Idiot, what are you doing?
- I got the green light!

108
00:10:30,756 --> 00:10:32,966
Don't you love
New York in the fall?

109
00:10:41,225 --> 00:10:42,434
Perfect.

110
00:10:50,817 --> 00:10:51,818
Can't beat that.

111
00:10:52,069 --> 00:10:54,780
Scotch tape?
What is going on with you?

112
00:10:55,197 --> 00:10:56,532
Nothing.

113
00:10:57,282 --> 00:10:59,868
- You're in love.
- In love? No.

114
00:11:01,161 --> 00:11:05,165
Yes, that's right!
I'm in love with Frank.

115
00:11:05,415 --> 00:11:08,210
I'm practically living with Frank.

116
00:11:09,711 --> 00:11:12,256
Could you get our
Christmas mailers out this week?

117
00:11:12,506 --> 00:11:16,969
By Monday, I promise.
I have this paper due Friday.

118
00:11:19,388 --> 00:11:20,722
What is going on?

119
00:11:21,557 --> 00:11:23,225
Nothing at all.

120
00:11:23,684 --> 00:11:27,771
You know, I am just going to
stand here until you tell me.

121
00:11:28,522 --> 00:11:29,481
All right.

122
00:11:29,815 --> 00:11:32,860
Is it infidelity if you're involved
with someone on e-mail?

123
00:11:33,068 --> 00:11:34,570
Have you had sex?

124
00:11:34,820 --> 00:11:36,530
No, I don't even know him.

125
00:11:36,738 --> 00:11:38,365
I mean cybersex.

126
00:11:39,366 --> 00:11:44,163
Don't do it. The minute you do,
they lose all respect for you.

127
00:11:44,955 --> 00:11:46,832
It's not like that.

128
00:11:47,082 --> 00:11:48,709
We just e-mail.
It's really nothing.

129
00:11:48,959 --> 00:11:53,046
On top of which, I'm thinking of
stopping because it's getting...

130
00:11:53,297 --> 00:11:54,631
Out of hand?

131
00:11:55,465 --> 00:11:56,967
Confusing.

132
00:11:57,259 --> 00:11:58,135
But not.

133
00:11:58,385 --> 00:11:59,803
Because it's nothing.

134
00:12:00,053 --> 00:12:01,138
Where'd you meet him?

135
00:12:01,388 --> 00:12:03,473
Listen, I can't even remember.

136
00:12:09,438 --> 00:12:13,775
On my birthday, I wandered into
the "over 30" room for a joke, sort of.

137
00:12:14,401 --> 00:12:16,486
And he was there.
And we started chatting.

138
00:12:16,695 --> 00:12:18,071
About what?

139
00:12:18,822 --> 00:12:22,159
Books and music,
how much we both love New York...

140
00:12:22,409 --> 00:12:23,619
Harmless, harmless.

141
00:12:23,869 --> 00:12:25,204
Meaningless.

142
00:12:25,621 --> 00:12:28,123
Bouquets of sharpened pencils.

143
00:12:28,707 --> 00:12:29,708
Excuse me?

144
00:12:30,083 --> 00:12:30,918
Forget it.

145
00:12:31,168 --> 00:12:34,671
We don't talk about anything personal,
so I don't know his name...

146
00:12:34,922 --> 00:12:37,007
...or what he does
or where he lives exactly...

147
00:12:37,257 --> 00:12:41,762
...so it'll be easy for me to stop
seeing him, because I'm not.

148
00:12:42,429 --> 00:12:45,641
He could be the next person
to walk into the store.

149
00:12:45,891 --> 00:12:46,725
I know.

150
00:12:46,975 --> 00:12:48,018
He could be...

151
00:12:51,396 --> 00:12:52,856
...George.

152
00:12:55,275 --> 00:12:56,360
Morning.

153
00:12:56,652 --> 00:12:58,612
Are you online?

154
00:12:58,862 --> 00:13:00,781
As far as I'm concerned...

155
00:13:01,031 --> 00:13:05,035
...the Internet is just another way
of being rejected by a woman.

156
00:13:08,914 --> 00:13:10,499
Good morning, Birdie.

157
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,919
What are you girls talking about?

158
00:13:14,336 --> 00:13:15,420
Cybersex.

159
00:13:15,754 --> 00:13:18,882
I tried once, but I kept
getting a busy signal.

160
00:13:19,174 --> 00:13:20,050
I know.

161
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:23,220
I was really depressed
one Saturday night about 9:00...

162
00:13:23,595 --> 00:13:25,472
Time to open up!

163
00:13:29,768 --> 00:13:32,104
Jessica and Maya, how are you today?

164
00:13:32,437 --> 00:13:35,732
- Want to say hi to Kathleen?
- Hi, Kathleen.

165
00:13:37,150 --> 00:13:38,610
May I help you?

166
00:13:40,737 --> 00:13:42,823
Construction's going well.
We should open on time.

167
00:13:43,073 --> 00:13:47,411
Although Kevin and I are concerned
about the neighborhood response.

168
00:13:47,661 --> 00:13:50,664
This fabric on the couch,
what is it?

169
00:13:50,998 --> 00:13:52,249
Money.

170
00:13:53,083 --> 00:13:54,918
Its name is money.

171
00:13:55,127 --> 00:13:57,462
- Gillian selected it.
- Good guess.

172
00:13:57,671 --> 00:14:00,257
- Your father's getting married again.
- Really?

173
00:14:00,465 --> 00:14:01,800
Congratulations!

174
00:14:02,050 --> 00:14:02,676
Why?

175
00:14:02,926 --> 00:14:03,677
Who knows?

176
00:14:03,927 --> 00:14:04,511
Love?

177
00:14:04,761 --> 00:14:05,637
Possible.

178
00:14:05,888 --> 00:14:07,431
You're a damn fool!

179
00:14:07,681 --> 00:14:09,766
Pops, Matthew is
four years old, okay?

180
00:14:10,017 --> 00:14:12,519
It'd be nice
if his parents were married.

181
00:14:12,978 --> 00:14:15,814
Listen, I have a
sad announcement to make.

182
00:14:16,607 --> 00:14:17,816
City Books...

183
00:14:18,066 --> 00:14:19,276
...on 23rd Street.

184
00:14:19,526 --> 00:14:20,694
It's going under.

185
00:14:26,033 --> 00:14:28,076
Another independent bites the dust.

186
00:14:28,327 --> 00:14:29,661
On to the next!

187
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:33,290
Going to buy out their
inventory of architecture...

188
00:14:33,540 --> 00:14:35,209
...and New York history
for the new store.

189
00:14:35,459 --> 00:14:37,211
How much you paying, son?

190
00:14:37,461 --> 00:14:41,632
It won't be as much as that
uncomfortable mohair episode there...

191
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:44,343
...which is now all over my suit.

192
00:14:44,593 --> 00:14:45,761
Here you go.

193
00:14:47,346 --> 00:14:51,350
We'll also have a section dedicated
to writers from the West Side.

194
00:14:51,600 --> 00:14:53,143
As a sop to the neighborhood.

195
00:14:53,393 --> 00:14:56,647
Keep those West Side,
liberal nuts, pseudo-intellectual...

196
00:14:56,897 --> 00:14:59,107
Readers, Dad.
They're called readers.

197
00:14:59,358 --> 00:15:01,860
Don't romanticize them.
It'll keep them...

198
00:15:02,110 --> 00:15:03,654
...from jumping down your throat.

199
00:15:03,904 --> 00:15:04,821
What's the competition?

200
00:15:05,447 --> 00:15:09,201
One mystery store, Sleuth,
at 78th and Amsterdam...

201
00:15:09,451 --> 00:15:11,912
...and a children's bookstore,
Shop Around The Corner.

202
00:15:12,162 --> 00:15:14,873
- It's been there forever.
- Cecilia's store.

203
00:15:15,707 --> 00:15:16,583
Who's that?

204
00:15:17,459 --> 00:15:19,002
Cecilia Kelly.

205
00:15:19,253 --> 00:15:22,548
Lovely woman. I think we might
have had a date once.

206
00:15:22,965 --> 00:15:25,050
Or maybe we just exchanged letters.

207
00:15:26,343 --> 00:15:27,761
You wrote her letters?

208
00:15:28,428 --> 00:15:31,056
Mail. It was called mail.

209
00:15:31,265 --> 00:15:34,017
- Stamps, envelopes...
- I've heard of it.

210
00:15:34,268 --> 00:15:37,020
Cecilia had beautiful penmanship.

211
00:15:37,271 --> 00:15:39,731
She was too young for me...

212
00:15:40,232 --> 00:15:41,441
...but she was...

213
00:15:41,692 --> 00:15:42,734
...enchanting.

214
00:15:43,485 --> 00:15:44,236
Enchanting?

215
00:15:44,486 --> 00:15:46,154
Her daughter owns it now.

216
00:15:46,947 --> 00:15:48,574
Too bad for her.

217
00:15:51,451 --> 00:15:52,828
Excuse me, Mr. Fox.

218
00:15:53,078 --> 00:15:54,830
My father is getting married again.

219
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:57,583
For 5 years, he's been living
with Gillian...

220
00:15:57,833 --> 00:16:00,085
...who studied decorating
at Caesar's Palace.

221
00:16:00,335 --> 00:16:01,503
Is it porcelain?

222
00:16:02,171 --> 00:16:03,297
Rubber.

223
00:16:04,923 --> 00:16:07,634
Once I read a story about a
butterfly in the subway, and today...

224
00:16:08,260 --> 00:16:09,678
...I saw one.

225
00:16:09,928 --> 00:16:12,681
It got on at 42nd
and off at 59th...

226
00:16:12,931 --> 00:16:14,558
...where, I assume
it went to Bloomingdale's...

227
00:16:14,808 --> 00:16:16,894
...to buy a hat that will
turn out to be a mistake.

228
00:16:17,144 --> 00:16:19,354
As almost all hats are.

229
00:16:21,190 --> 00:16:22,024
Listen to this:

230
00:16:22,274 --> 00:16:24,985
Every night a truck pulls up
to my neighborhood bagel place...

231
00:16:25,235 --> 00:16:28,030
...and pumps a ton of flour
into underground tanks.

232
00:16:28,280 --> 00:16:32,117
The air is filled with white dust
which never seems to land.

233
00:16:32,367 --> 00:16:33,911
Why is that?

234
00:16:35,078 --> 00:16:39,833
Confession: I've read
Pride and Prejudice about 200 times.

235
00:16:40,334 --> 00:16:42,419
I get lost in the language.
Words like...

236
00:16:42,669 --> 00:16:43,754
..."thither".

237
00:16:44,004 --> 00:16:45,797
"Mischance."

238
00:16:46,048 --> 00:16:47,758
"Felicity."

239
00:16:48,467 --> 00:16:51,011
I'm always in agony over whether
Elizabeth and Mr. Darcy...

240
00:16:51,261 --> 00:16:53,597
...are really going to get together.

241
00:16:53,847 --> 00:16:55,015
Read it.

242
00:16:55,265 --> 00:16:57,351
I know you'll love it.

243
00:16:58,810 --> 00:17:00,562
The purpose of places
like Starbucks...

244
00:17:00,812 --> 00:17:03,273
...is for people with
no decision-making ability...

245
00:17:03,524 --> 00:17:07,152
...to make six decisions
just to buy one cup of coffee.

246
00:17:07,444 --> 00:17:09,446
Short, tall, lite, dark...

247
00:17:09,696 --> 00:17:11,365
...caf, decaf...

248
00:17:11,615 --> 00:17:12,449
...low-fat, nonfat...

249
00:17:13,659 --> 00:17:15,077
...et cetera.

250
00:17:15,577 --> 00:17:17,246
Mocha frappuccino grande.

251
00:17:17,496 --> 00:17:19,915
So people who don 't know
what the hell they're doing...

252
00:17:20,165 --> 00:17:23,001
...or who on earth they are
can, for only $2.95...

253
00:17:23,252 --> 00:17:25,337
...get not just a cup of coffee...

254
00:17:25,587 --> 00:17:29,466
...but an absolutely
defining sense of self.

255
00:17:30,467 --> 00:17:31,552
Tall!

256
00:17:31,802 --> 00:17:32,803
Decaf!

257
00:17:33,053 --> 00:17:34,263
Cappuccino!

258
00:17:34,513 --> 00:17:36,181
Tall decaf cappuccino.

259
00:17:57,870 --> 00:17:59,204
Bummer!

260
00:18:01,373 --> 00:18:03,542
A Fox Books Superstore.

261
00:18:03,917 --> 00:18:06,170
Quel nightmare.

262
00:18:06,420 --> 00:18:08,755
It has nothing to do with us.
It's big...

263
00:18:09,006 --> 00:18:10,048
...impersonal...

264
00:18:10,299 --> 00:18:13,468
...overstocked and full
of ignorant salespeople.

265
00:18:13,719 --> 00:18:15,387
But they discount.

266
00:18:15,637 --> 00:18:17,598
But they don't provide any service.

267
00:18:17,848 --> 00:18:19,183
We do.

268
00:18:19,433 --> 00:18:20,684
So, really...

269
00:18:20,934 --> 00:18:22,561
...it's a good development.

270
00:18:22,811 --> 00:18:25,772
You know how in the flower district
there are all those shops...

271
00:18:26,023 --> 00:18:28,275
...so you can find whatever you want?

272
00:18:28,984 --> 00:18:31,695
This is going to be
the book district.

273
00:18:31,945 --> 00:18:33,447
If they don't have it, we do.

274
00:18:34,740 --> 00:18:36,033
And vice versa.

275
00:18:36,325 --> 00:18:37,326
Absolutely.

276
00:18:41,038 --> 00:18:43,248
When you are finished
with Fox Books...

277
00:18:43,499 --> 00:18:46,793
...The Shop Around The Corner
will be responsible...

278
00:18:47,044 --> 00:18:51,215
...for reversing the entire course
of the Industrial Revolution.

279
00:18:51,465 --> 00:18:54,218
Well, now, that is so sweet!

280
00:18:54,468 --> 00:18:57,137
Thank you. That is so sweet.

281
00:18:57,387 --> 00:18:58,222
Although I...

282
00:18:58,472 --> 00:18:59,223
What?

283
00:18:59,473 --> 00:19:01,183
Wait a minute.
What is that doing here?

284
00:19:01,433 --> 00:19:02,684
Oh, my gosh!

285
00:19:02,935 --> 00:19:04,394
This is amazing.

286
00:19:04,645 --> 00:19:05,812
Listen.

287
00:19:07,773 --> 00:19:10,108
The Olympia Report Deluxe Electric.

288
00:19:10,442 --> 00:19:11,401
Report.

289
00:19:11,693 --> 00:19:12,986
As in gunshot.

290
00:19:13,237 --> 00:19:15,781
- That sound is familiar.
- Listen to this.

291
00:19:16,782 --> 00:19:18,325
What, that whirring?

292
00:19:18,575 --> 00:19:20,786
The gentle, soothing lullaby...

293
00:19:21,036 --> 00:19:23,539
...of a piece of machinery so perfect...

294
00:19:23,789 --> 00:19:25,958
I know where I've heard that before.

295
00:19:27,668 --> 00:19:28,502
I needed a backup.

296
00:19:28,752 --> 00:19:30,754
Don't you have another one
at your apartment?

297
00:19:31,004 --> 00:19:32,881
- I might!
- That you wrote a column about?

298
00:19:33,131 --> 00:19:34,091
Who cares?

299
00:19:35,133 --> 00:19:36,552
What were you going to say?

300
00:19:36,802 --> 00:19:37,636
- When?
- Before.

301
00:19:37,886 --> 00:19:38,720
Nothing.

302
00:19:39,638 --> 00:19:40,430
Come on.

303
00:19:42,641 --> 00:19:46,144
I'm just wondering.
I'm wondering about my work.

304
00:19:47,604 --> 00:19:51,608
What is it that I do, exactly?
All I really do is...

305
00:19:51,859 --> 00:19:53,443
All you really do...

306
00:19:53,694 --> 00:19:55,779
...is this incredibly noble thing.

307
00:19:56,029 --> 00:19:56,864
I don't know.

308
00:19:57,114 --> 00:19:59,324
Really, I'm just...

309
00:20:00,367 --> 00:20:02,119
You are a lone reed.

310
00:20:03,745 --> 00:20:05,205
You are a lone...

311
00:20:07,040 --> 00:20:08,208
...reed...

312
00:20:08,458 --> 00:20:09,960
...standing...

313
00:20:10,210 --> 00:20:11,545
...tall...

314
00:20:11,795 --> 00:20:13,797
...waving boldly...

315
00:20:14,339 --> 00:20:16,466
...in the corrupt sands...

316
00:20:16,717 --> 00:20:18,135
...of commerce.

317
00:20:21,513 --> 00:20:23,182
I am a lone reed.

318
00:20:25,058 --> 00:20:27,895
I am a lone reed.

319
00:20:34,026 --> 00:20:36,778
Sometimes I wonder about my life.

320
00:20:37,029 --> 00:20:39,198
I lead a small life.

321
00:20:39,531 --> 00:20:41,533
Well, valuable, but small.

322
00:20:41,783 --> 00:20:43,577
And sometimes I wonder...

323
00:20:43,785 --> 00:20:45,829
...do I do it because I like it...

324
00:20:46,038 --> 00:20:48,415
...or because I haven 't been brave?

325
00:20:49,333 --> 00:20:52,669
So much of what I see reminds me
of something in a book...

326
00:20:52,920 --> 00:20:55,172
...when shouldn't it be
the other way around?

327
00:20:56,256 --> 00:20:58,509
I don 't want an answer.
I just want...

328
00:20:58,717 --> 00:21:01,595
...to send this cosmic question
into the void.

329
00:21:02,054 --> 00:21:03,430
So...

330
00:21:03,639 --> 00:21:05,766
...good night, dear void.

331
00:21:15,526 --> 00:21:16,985
I know you!

332
00:21:20,030 --> 00:21:21,865
Hello, Annabel, little girl.

333
00:21:22,449 --> 00:21:23,492
How are you?

334
00:21:23,700 --> 00:21:24,743
And you!

335
00:21:25,077 --> 00:21:26,620
Matthew, how are you?

336
00:21:27,079 --> 00:21:28,038
Good.

337
00:21:28,372 --> 00:21:29,957
Ready to say hello to New Jersey?

338
00:21:30,249 --> 00:21:32,584
Hello, New Jersey!

339
00:21:34,753 --> 00:21:35,671
Don't I get a "hello"?

340
00:21:37,464 --> 00:21:38,382
Hello, Gillian.

341
00:21:39,299 --> 00:21:41,885
Kiss me. I'll be
your wicked stepmother.

342
00:21:45,180 --> 00:21:46,056
There you go.

343
00:21:46,306 --> 00:21:47,933
Hello. And who is this?

344
00:21:48,976 --> 00:21:51,770
Nanny Maureen. In case
you couldn't handle the kids.

345
00:21:52,396 --> 00:21:53,981
Maureen's getting a divorce.

346
00:21:55,482 --> 00:21:56,483
I'm sorry to hear that.

347
00:21:57,150 --> 00:22:01,488
It's my own fault.
Never marry a man who lies.

348
00:22:02,072 --> 00:22:04,658
That is so wise.
Annabel, remember that.

349
00:22:05,159 --> 00:22:06,493
She taught Matt to spell his name.

350
00:22:07,119 --> 00:22:08,036
Really?

351
00:22:08,287 --> 00:22:09,830
- Let's hear it.
- F-O-X.

352
00:22:10,080 --> 00:22:11,415
Excellent!

353
00:22:11,832 --> 00:22:15,043
I've got this covered.
You can have the day off.

354
00:22:15,252 --> 00:22:18,839
And you must be late for something.
Volunteering, rolling bandages...

355
00:22:19,089 --> 00:22:20,465
...for Bosnian refugees.

356
00:22:20,841 --> 00:22:23,927
I am. I'm having
my eggs harvested.

357
00:22:25,596 --> 00:22:28,056
And getting those eggs harvested.
Don't worry!

358
00:22:28,307 --> 00:22:29,349
See you later.

359
00:22:30,392 --> 00:22:31,393
Bye, Mom.

360
00:22:31,727 --> 00:22:35,689
All right, you guys, are you ready
to go out on the boat?

361
00:22:37,024 --> 00:22:39,067
What happened to you?

362
00:22:55,042 --> 00:22:56,376
Come on, one more time.

363
00:22:56,627 --> 00:22:58,295
Here, you can do that one.

364
00:23:02,299 --> 00:23:03,800
What do we win?

365
00:23:15,854 --> 00:23:17,147
Look, look, look!

366
00:23:52,015 --> 00:23:54,643
- How are the fish?
- Very well, very happy.

367
00:23:54,977 --> 00:23:56,144
Are they?

368
00:23:56,395 --> 00:23:58,230
You guys want to go to a movie?

369
00:23:58,480 --> 00:24:00,107
There's nothing good playing.

370
00:24:00,399 --> 00:24:02,609
Look! The storybook lady.

371
00:24:03,193 --> 00:24:06,238
Are we at the right time?
Yeah, let's go.

372
00:24:06,572 --> 00:24:09,116
"That it was I and I alone who had...

373
00:24:09,366 --> 00:24:12,911
...the idea for the
great and daring mouse plot.

374
00:24:13,245 --> 00:24:15,372
We all have our moments
of brilliance and glory...

375
00:24:15,581 --> 00:24:17,875
...and this was mine.

376
00:24:18,125 --> 00:24:19,126
"Why don't we", I said...

377
00:24:19,376 --> 00:24:23,130
..."slip it into one of
Mrs. Pratchett's jars of sweets?

378
00:24:23,463 --> 00:24:24,464
And then...

379
00:24:24,715 --> 00:24:27,384
...when she puts her hand in
to grab a handful...

380
00:24:27,634 --> 00:24:32,264
...she will grab a stinky
dead mouse instead."

381
00:24:32,472 --> 00:24:34,600
The other four stared at me in wonder.

382
00:24:34,850 --> 00:24:38,937
Then, as the sheer genius
of the plot began to sink in...

383
00:24:39,188 --> 00:24:41,231
...they all started grinning.

384
00:24:41,481 --> 00:24:45,402
They slapped me on the back, cheered
and danced around the classroom.

385
00:24:45,652 --> 00:24:47,237
"We will do it today!" they cried.

386
00:24:47,487 --> 00:24:50,449
"We'll do it on the way home.
You had the idea", they said to me.

387
00:24:50,699 --> 00:24:52,951
"So you can be the one who
puts the mouse in the jar."

388
00:24:56,705 --> 00:24:59,583
- Who belongs to this fish?
- That's mine.

389
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,252
Could you help me with these books?

390
00:25:03,128 --> 00:25:06,423
And this is her best friend Tacy,
whose real name is Anastasia.

391
00:25:06,673 --> 00:25:09,927
And the next book, Betsy and Tacy
become friends with Tib...

392
00:25:10,177 --> 00:25:13,722
...whose real name, I am sorry
to tell you, is Thelma.

393
00:25:15,224 --> 00:25:17,893
The illustrations are hand-tipped.

394
00:25:18,685 --> 00:25:20,729
And that's why it costs so much?

395
00:25:21,188 --> 00:25:23,273
That's why it's worth so much.

396
00:25:23,482 --> 00:25:25,150
I want all of them.

397
00:25:25,400 --> 00:25:26,443
I'll think about it.

398
00:25:26,693 --> 00:25:28,445
That's a lot for your dad
to buy at one time.

399
00:25:28,820 --> 00:25:30,948
My dad gets me all the books I want.

400
00:25:31,198 --> 00:25:33,075
That's very nice of him.

401
00:25:33,283 --> 00:25:34,827
That's not my dad.
That's my nephew.

402
00:25:36,119 --> 00:25:39,498
I don't really think
that he could be your nephew.

403
00:25:39,957 --> 00:25:40,749
It's true.

404
00:25:40,999 --> 00:25:45,754
Annabel is my aunt.
Isn't that right, Aunt Annabel?

405
00:25:47,130 --> 00:25:47,965
And Matt is his...

406
00:25:48,215 --> 00:25:50,259
Wait, let me guess.

407
00:25:50,634 --> 00:25:52,427
Are you his uncle?

408
00:25:54,179 --> 00:25:55,556
His grandfather?

409
00:25:57,182 --> 00:25:58,600
His great-grandfather?

410
00:25:59,059 --> 00:26:00,519
I'm his brother!

411
00:26:02,479 --> 00:26:06,316
Matt is my father's son.
Annabel is my grandfather's daughter.

412
00:26:07,359 --> 00:26:08,861
We are...

413
00:26:09,695 --> 00:26:11,405
...an American family.

414
00:26:17,286 --> 00:26:18,370
Here you go.

415
00:26:18,579 --> 00:26:21,415
- What's that?
- That's a handkerchief.

416
00:26:21,707 --> 00:26:23,792
Do children not know
what handkerchiefs are?

417
00:26:24,042 --> 00:26:27,045
A handkerchief is a Kleenex
you don't throw away. See?

418
00:26:28,172 --> 00:26:29,631
My mother embroidered this.

419
00:26:29,882 --> 00:26:33,343
My initials and a daisy, because
daisies are my favorite flower.

420
00:26:34,469 --> 00:26:36,221
May I ask who you are?

421
00:26:37,014 --> 00:26:40,267
Kathleen Kelly. I own this store.
And you are?

422
00:26:41,602 --> 00:26:43,478
Just call me Joe.

423
00:26:43,854 --> 00:26:45,105
We'll take these.

424
00:26:47,441 --> 00:26:49,026
You're going to come back,
aren't you?

425
00:26:51,570 --> 00:26:52,571
Of course.

426
00:26:52,821 --> 00:26:53,530
See?

427
00:26:53,780 --> 00:26:56,658
That is why we won't go under.
Our customers are loyal.

428
00:26:56,909 --> 00:26:58,952
They're opening a Fox Books
around the corner.

429
00:26:59,286 --> 00:27:00,120
Fox Books!

430
00:27:00,370 --> 00:27:02,289
- My daddy...
- Likes to buy discount.

431
00:27:02,539 --> 00:27:04,458
Don't tell!
It's nothing to be proud of.

432
00:27:05,083 --> 00:27:06,710
F-O-X.

433
00:27:07,002 --> 00:27:09,421
That's amazing!
You can spell fox.

434
00:27:10,380 --> 00:27:12,466
Can you spell dog?

435
00:27:12,758 --> 00:27:13,842
F-O-X.

436
00:27:14,092 --> 00:27:16,720
Look at this pop-up dinosaur book.

437
00:27:16,929 --> 00:27:19,431
Wouldn't you like to have
a dinosaur book like that?

438
00:27:19,681 --> 00:27:22,142
Wouldn't you like to read that?

439
00:27:22,392 --> 00:27:25,729
Sit here and read Matt the book
until I take care of things.

440
00:27:25,979 --> 00:27:30,400
Whatever you do, just don't listen
to anything I say. Thank you.

441
00:27:31,151 --> 00:27:33,529
We'll take that pop-up book as well.

442
00:27:33,779 --> 00:27:37,825
The world is not driven by discounts.
I've been in business forever.

443
00:27:38,075 --> 00:27:42,246
I started helping my mother
when I was six, and I watched her.

444
00:27:42,913 --> 00:27:45,457
She wasn't just selling books.

445
00:27:45,666 --> 00:27:48,669
She was helping people become
what they were going to be.

446
00:27:48,919 --> 00:27:51,296
When you read a book as a child,
it becomes part of your identity...

447
00:27:51,547 --> 00:27:54,258
...in a way that no other reading
in your whole life does.

448
00:27:54,508 --> 00:27:56,426
And I have gotten carried away.

449
00:27:59,680 --> 00:28:01,306
Yeah, you have.

450
00:28:01,807 --> 00:28:03,809
You've made me feel...

451
00:28:06,770 --> 00:28:09,398
Enchanting.
Your mother was enchanting.

452
00:28:09,648 --> 00:28:10,732
Yes, she was.

453
00:28:11,191 --> 00:28:12,442
How will you pay?

454
00:28:13,193 --> 00:28:14,027
Cash.

455
00:28:14,736 --> 00:28:15,863
How did you know that?

456
00:28:16,905 --> 00:28:18,031
From the photograph.

457
00:28:19,158 --> 00:28:21,326
That you in the photograph?
What are you doing?

458
00:28:21,702 --> 00:28:22,953
Twirling.

459
00:28:24,288 --> 00:28:26,290
My mother and I used to twirl.

460
00:28:26,540 --> 00:28:30,169
She left the store to me,
and I'll leave it to my daughter.

461
00:28:30,419 --> 00:28:31,920
- $73, please.
- How much?

462
00:28:32,171 --> 00:28:33,172
$73.

463
00:28:36,508 --> 00:28:39,469
How old is your daughter now?

464
00:28:40,888 --> 00:28:42,472
I don't have a daughter.

465
00:28:42,931 --> 00:28:45,475
I'm not married. But eventually...

466
00:28:45,684 --> 00:28:49,021
So the big bad Fox Books
can just go to hell.

467
00:28:50,981 --> 00:28:51,857
Here you go.

468
00:28:52,149 --> 00:28:54,193
Thank you. We're ready?

469
00:28:55,819 --> 00:28:57,362
This is nice.

470
00:28:57,988 --> 00:29:01,033
Goodbye, Annabel.
Goodbye, Matt.

471
00:29:04,995 --> 00:29:07,623
Matt, I have to ask you
another thing.

472
00:29:07,831 --> 00:29:09,291
Can you spell cat?

473
00:29:09,541 --> 00:29:11,376
F-O-X.

474
00:29:13,837 --> 00:29:14,671
Cat!

475
00:29:14,880 --> 00:29:16,048
Thanks.

476
00:29:19,051 --> 00:29:20,344
Good thing it wasn't the fish.

477
00:29:22,763 --> 00:29:24,181
Take care!

478
00:30:11,395 --> 00:30:14,189
No protests, no demonstrations.

479
00:30:14,398 --> 00:30:15,941
The neighborhood loves us.

480
00:30:16,149 --> 00:30:18,151
They're wondering where
we've been all these years...

481
00:30:18,402 --> 00:30:19,903
...how they did without us.

482
00:30:20,153 --> 00:30:21,071
It's a hit!

483
00:30:21,530 --> 00:30:23,031
How's the children's department?

484
00:30:23,282 --> 00:30:25,117
It's early. School's not out.

485
00:30:25,367 --> 00:30:28,662
And there is that
children's store nearby.

486
00:30:28,871 --> 00:30:31,290
Cecilia's store. Cecilia Kelly.

487
00:30:31,623 --> 00:30:33,292
We might have had a date once.

488
00:30:33,584 --> 00:30:36,044
- Her daughter owns it now.
- We'll crush it!

489
00:30:36,295 --> 00:30:38,005
She was enchanting.

490
00:30:43,468 --> 00:30:46,054
They've been open 6 days.

491
00:30:46,263 --> 00:30:48,098
And we did $1200 less...

492
00:30:48,849 --> 00:30:50,767
...than the same week last year.

493
00:30:51,018 --> 00:30:52,477
That could be a fluke, right?

494
00:30:53,520 --> 00:30:54,479
Or not.

495
00:30:55,898 --> 00:30:58,525
Their store is new.
It's a novelty.

496
00:30:58,817 --> 00:31:01,945
It'll all shake out.
Meanwhile, I'll put up more lights.

497
00:31:02,696 --> 00:31:03,989
That's a fine idea.

498
00:31:04,448 --> 00:31:06,241
What if we have to fold?

499
00:31:06,492 --> 00:31:08,827
I'll never find
another part-time job.

500
00:31:09,077 --> 00:31:12,414
Then I won't be able to pay
my rent, and I'll have to move.

501
00:31:12,664 --> 00:31:13,957
To Brooklyn!

502
00:31:14,208 --> 00:31:17,127
The joy of rent control.
Six rooms...

503
00:31:17,377 --> 00:31:18,754
...$450 a month.

504
00:31:19,004 --> 00:31:21,089
You've told us a million times.

505
00:31:21,340 --> 00:31:24,801
I can't believe you're bringing
this up at a time like this.

506
00:31:25,010 --> 00:31:28,347
It's like those people who brag
because they're tall.

507
00:31:28,555 --> 00:31:30,474
We are not going to fold.

508
00:31:33,560 --> 00:31:35,062
This place is a tomb.

509
00:31:35,312 --> 00:31:38,065
I'm going to the nut shop,
where it's fun.

510
00:31:50,744 --> 00:31:51,537
Miranda!

511
00:31:55,457 --> 00:31:58,252
Kathleen, are you surviving?

512
00:31:58,585 --> 00:32:02,214
We're excited about your new book.
When should we schedule a signing?

513
00:32:02,756 --> 00:32:06,635
It's being published in January.
Will you be in business in January?

514
00:32:06,885 --> 00:32:08,095
I'm so worried.

515
00:32:08,387 --> 00:32:09,638
We're doing great.

516
00:32:09,847 --> 00:32:11,014
Aren't we?

517
00:32:11,265 --> 00:32:13,100
No difference whatsoever!

518
00:32:13,350 --> 00:32:14,393
Great.

519
00:32:14,726 --> 00:32:15,853
Thank God!

520
00:32:16,520 --> 00:32:20,649
You can count on me for anything.
Support, rallies, picket lines.

521
00:32:20,899 --> 00:32:22,985
We can get the Times
to write something.

522
00:32:23,861 --> 00:32:25,404
Or that nut from the Observer.

523
00:32:27,364 --> 00:32:30,284
- What nut in the "Observer"?
- Frank something-or-other.

524
00:32:30,534 --> 00:32:32,494
The one who's in love
with his typewriter.

525
00:32:32,703 --> 00:32:35,247
It's just the sort of thing
to outrage him.

526
00:32:38,083 --> 00:32:39,668
She called me a nut?

527
00:32:40,210 --> 00:32:42,838
That's not the point.
She thinks my store is in trouble.

528
00:32:43,088 --> 00:32:45,799
Why'd she say that?
There's enough business for us all.

529
00:32:46,008 --> 00:32:47,342
Yes, there is.

530
00:32:47,593 --> 00:32:48,552
We are fine.

531
00:32:48,802 --> 00:32:50,804
You're more than fine.
You're absolutely fine.

532
00:32:51,096 --> 00:32:52,431
We are fine.

533
00:32:52,764 --> 00:32:54,057
How are you?

534
00:32:54,308 --> 00:32:55,350
Fine.

535
00:32:55,684 --> 00:32:58,103
Vince will be so happy to see you.

536
00:32:58,562 --> 00:33:00,647
Congratulations!

537
00:33:07,070 --> 00:33:09,072
This is amazing.
He said:

538
00:33:09,323 --> 00:33:11,408
"You should go to a retreat.

539
00:33:11,658 --> 00:33:14,328
You really should go to a retreat."

540
00:33:18,248 --> 00:33:20,250
Honey, will you get me
another drink?

541
00:33:20,501 --> 00:33:22,002
"It's a great place...

542
00:33:22,753 --> 00:33:25,047
...to calm down."

543
00:33:25,297 --> 00:33:27,174
He said that.
Isn't that hilarious?

544
00:33:32,429 --> 00:33:33,931
Champagne, please.

545
00:33:34,723 --> 00:33:38,310
Stoli on the rocks.
But a fresh glass, please.

546
00:33:38,685 --> 00:33:40,229
White wine, please.

547
00:33:40,521 --> 00:33:41,813
Here you go.

548
00:33:51,990 --> 00:33:53,408
Do you remember me?

549
00:33:53,617 --> 00:33:55,744
Of course I remember you.

550
00:33:56,245 --> 00:33:57,704
How's your aunt?

551
00:33:58,497 --> 00:33:59,748
She's great. Thanks.

552
00:33:59,998 --> 00:34:01,458
I'd better go deliver this.

553
00:34:01,708 --> 00:34:04,336
I have a thirsty date.
She's part camel.

554
00:34:04,586 --> 00:34:06,588
Joe, right? Joe, isn't it?

555
00:34:07,464 --> 00:34:09,049
And you are Kathleen.

556
00:34:14,555 --> 00:34:15,639
Kathleen Kelly.

557
00:34:15,889 --> 00:34:17,349
Two white wine, please.

558
00:34:22,563 --> 00:34:26,483
I cannot believe that
you were speaking to Joe Fox.

559
00:34:26,817 --> 00:34:28,443
Joe Fox?

560
00:34:29,486 --> 00:34:30,154
As in...

561
00:34:30,404 --> 00:34:33,407
As in, he's going to
take over everything.

562
00:34:41,582 --> 00:34:43,709
Fox? Your last name is Fox.

563
00:34:44,877 --> 00:34:46,420
F-O-X.

564
00:34:47,838 --> 00:34:48,755
God!

565
00:34:48,964 --> 00:34:50,382
I didn't realize...

566
00:34:50,632 --> 00:34:52,259
I didn't know...

567
00:34:53,010 --> 00:34:55,721
Who you were with?
I didn't know who you were with.

568
00:34:56,054 --> 00:34:57,514
Excuse me?

569
00:34:58,307 --> 00:35:00,100
It's from The Godfather.

570
00:35:00,475 --> 00:35:02,102
Sorry, it's from The Godfather.

571
00:35:02,352 --> 00:35:05,731
It's when the movie producer
realizes that Tom Hagen...

572
00:35:05,981 --> 00:35:08,525
...is an emissary of Vito Corleone.

573
00:35:08,775 --> 00:35:10,694
Just before the horse's head
ends up in the bed...

574
00:35:10,944 --> 00:35:13,697
...with all the bloody sheets.
Wakes up and it's...

575
00:35:17,868 --> 00:35:18,911
Never mind.

576
00:35:19,203 --> 00:35:23,415
You were spying on me, weren't you?
You probably rented those children.

577
00:35:23,624 --> 00:35:24,416
Why would I spy on you?

578
00:35:24,666 --> 00:35:27,127
Because I am your competition,
which you know...

579
00:35:27,377 --> 00:35:31,215
...or you wouldn't have put up the sign:
"Just around the corner."

580
00:35:31,924 --> 00:35:36,178
Our store entrance is around the corner.
There's no other way to say it.

581
00:35:36,428 --> 00:35:39,515
It's not the name of our store.
It's where it is.

582
00:35:39,765 --> 00:35:43,393
And you do not own the phrase
"around the corner".

583
00:35:46,480 --> 00:35:47,648
What is that?

584
00:35:48,065 --> 00:35:49,066
What are you doing?

585
00:35:49,858 --> 00:35:51,318
What are you doing?

586
00:35:51,819 --> 00:35:54,780
You're taking all the caviar?
That caviar is a garnish.

587
00:36:01,036 --> 00:36:03,372
I came into your store because...

588
00:36:03,580 --> 00:36:06,458
...I was spending the day
with Annabel and Matt.

589
00:36:06,708 --> 00:36:08,043
I was buying them presents.

590
00:36:08,293 --> 00:36:12,548
I'm the type of guy who buys his way
into the hearts of children.

591
00:36:12,756 --> 00:36:16,218
There was only one place to find
a children's book in the neighborhood.

592
00:36:16,468 --> 00:36:17,636
That won't always be the case.

593
00:36:17,886 --> 00:36:19,638
And it was yours.
And it is...

594
00:36:19,972 --> 00:36:21,932
...a charming little bookstore.

595
00:36:22,140 --> 00:36:25,644
You probably sell, what,
$350,000 worth of books in a year.

596
00:36:25,894 --> 00:36:27,062
How did you know that?

597
00:36:27,396 --> 00:36:28,522
I'm in the book business.

598
00:36:29,690 --> 00:36:31,191
I am in the book business.

599
00:36:33,152 --> 00:36:34,111
I see.

600
00:36:35,070 --> 00:36:36,655
And we are the Price Club.

601
00:36:36,905 --> 00:36:40,325
Only instead of a ten-gallon vat
of olive oil for $3.99...

602
00:36:40,576 --> 00:36:44,288
...that won't even fit in your
cabinet, we sell cheap books.

603
00:36:44,538 --> 00:36:46,915
Me, a spy? Absolutely!

604
00:36:47,166 --> 00:36:51,587
I have in my possession the secret
printout of the sales figures...

605
00:36:51,837 --> 00:36:54,840
...of a bookstore so inconsequential,
yet full of its own virtue...

606
00:36:55,090 --> 00:36:58,844
...that I had to rush over for fear
it will put me out of business.

607
00:37:01,847 --> 00:37:03,390
What?

608
00:37:03,891 --> 00:37:05,601
How you doing? Frank Navasky.

609
00:37:06,852 --> 00:37:07,728
Joe Fox.

610
00:37:11,773 --> 00:37:14,026
The inventor of the superstore.

611
00:37:14,276 --> 00:37:16,111
The enemy of the mid-list novel.

612
00:37:16,361 --> 00:37:17,654
The destroyer of City Books.

613
00:37:17,905 --> 00:37:19,740
How do you sleep at night?

614
00:37:20,741 --> 00:37:23,744
I use a wonderful over-the-counter drug.

615
00:37:23,994 --> 00:37:24,912
"Ultra-dorm."

616
00:37:25,496 --> 00:37:27,164
Just take a half.

617
00:37:27,414 --> 00:37:29,458
You'll wake without
even a tiny hangover.

618
00:37:30,167 --> 00:37:31,877
You're Frank Navasky, aren't you?

619
00:37:32,127 --> 00:37:34,296
Your last piece in the Observer...

620
00:37:34,546 --> 00:37:37,090
...about Anthony Powell was brilliant.

621
00:37:38,425 --> 00:37:39,468
Brilliant!

622
00:37:40,552 --> 00:37:41,887
I'm Patricia Eden.

623
00:37:42,137 --> 00:37:43,639
Eden Books.

624
00:37:44,014 --> 00:37:47,100
This man is the greatest
living expert...

625
00:37:47,309 --> 00:37:48,769
...on Julius and Ethel Rosenberg.

626
00:37:49,019 --> 00:37:49,978
You really liked...

627
00:37:50,604 --> 00:37:52,064
This is Kathleen Kelly.

628
00:37:52,272 --> 00:37:55,692
My piece? I just...
I'm flattered.

629
00:37:55,943 --> 00:37:58,153
You write these things.
You think somebody'll mention them.

630
00:37:58,403 --> 00:38:00,531
The phone doesn't ring
and you think:

631
00:38:00,864 --> 00:38:03,367
"I'm a fraud. I'm a failure."

632
00:38:03,617 --> 00:38:06,495
You know what fascinated me about
Julius and Ethel Rosenberg?

633
00:38:06,745 --> 00:38:10,123
Is how old they looked
when they were really just our age.

634
00:38:11,708 --> 00:38:12,668
You know?

635
00:38:13,210 --> 00:38:15,128
I'm so happy to have
finally met you.

636
00:38:16,797 --> 00:38:19,341
Have you ever
thought about doing a book?

637
00:38:19,591 --> 00:38:21,552
It's crossed my mind,
something relevant for today...

638
00:38:21,802 --> 00:38:24,721
...like the Luddite movement in
19th century England. We should talk.

639
00:38:24,972 --> 00:38:25,848
Call me.

640
00:38:26,098 --> 00:38:28,725
Have you ever had a caviar garnish?

641
00:38:34,731 --> 00:38:38,318
I had no idea Frank Navasky...

642
00:38:38,527 --> 00:38:40,571
...was going to be so down-to-earth.

643
00:38:41,238 --> 00:38:42,865
You read his stuff...

644
00:38:43,115 --> 00:38:45,826
...you think he's going to be
so obscure and abstruse.

645
00:38:46,076 --> 00:38:48,912
He's always talking
about Heidegger...

646
00:38:49,163 --> 00:38:50,539
...and Foucault.

647
00:38:50,789 --> 00:38:54,042
And I have no idea
what it's about, really.

648
00:39:00,799 --> 00:39:02,926
I'm not tired. I'm not.

649
00:39:18,483 --> 00:39:22,488
Do you ever feel you've become
the worst version of yourself?

650
00:39:26,492 --> 00:39:30,245
That a Pandora 's box of
all the secret, hateful parts...

651
00:39:30,496 --> 00:39:32,664
...your arrogance, your spite...

652
00:39:32,915 --> 00:39:34,249
...your condescension...

653
00:39:34,500 --> 00:39:36,210
...has sprung open?

654
00:39:36,418 --> 00:39:39,046
Someone provokes you,
and instead of smiling...

655
00:39:39,296 --> 00:39:42,174
...and moving on, you zing them?

656
00:39:42,424 --> 00:39:44,927
"Hello, it's Mr. Nasty."

657
00:39:45,761 --> 00:39:49,181
I'm sure you have no idea
what I'm talking about.

658
00:39:49,640 --> 00:39:52,935
I know what you mean,
and I'm completely jealous.

659
00:39:55,020 --> 00:39:59,483
When I'm provoked I get
tongue-tied. My mind goes...

660
00:39:59,983 --> 00:40:00,984
...blank.

661
00:40:04,071 --> 00:40:06,281
Then I toss and turn all night...

662
00:40:06,532 --> 00:40:08,992
...trying to figure out
what I should've said.

663
00:40:12,746 --> 00:40:14,498
What should I have said,
for example, to the...

664
00:40:14,790 --> 00:40:17,709
...bottom-dweller...

665
00:40:20,212 --> 00:40:23,841
...who recently belittled
my existence?

666
00:40:32,975 --> 00:40:34,101
Nothing.

667
00:40:35,060 --> 00:40:36,436
Nothing. Even now.

668
00:40:36,687 --> 00:40:37,771
Even now...

669
00:40:38,021 --> 00:40:38,856
...days later...

670
00:40:39,106 --> 00:40:40,524
...I can't figure it out.

671
00:40:41,066 --> 00:40:44,903
Wouldn't it be wonderful if I could
pass all my zingers to you?

672
00:40:45,154 --> 00:40:48,907
Then I'd never behave badly and
you could behave badly all the time.

673
00:40:49,158 --> 00:40:50,242
And we'd be happy.

674
00:40:50,492 --> 00:40:52,077
On the other hand,
I must warn you:

675
00:40:52,327 --> 00:40:55,539
When you finally say
the thing you mean to say...

676
00:40:55,789 --> 00:40:59,918
...at the moment you mean to say it,
remorse inevitably follows.

677
00:41:05,299 --> 00:41:07,926
Do you think we should meet?

678
00:41:18,103 --> 00:41:19,396
Meet?

679
00:41:19,980 --> 00:41:21,732
Oh, my God!

680
00:42:24,127 --> 00:42:25,838
$72.27.

681
00:42:28,006 --> 00:42:30,133
This is a cash-only line.

682
00:42:32,511 --> 00:42:33,512
Cash only.

683
00:42:34,346 --> 00:42:35,556
Oh, my God!

684
00:42:36,473 --> 00:42:39,643
I just have a credit card.
I'm sorry. Is that okay?

685
00:42:39,852 --> 00:42:41,270
No, it's not okay.

686
00:42:41,687 --> 00:42:42,813
There's a sign.

687
00:42:44,022 --> 00:42:45,816
I'm sorry. I'm very sorry.

688
00:42:46,066 --> 00:42:49,736
I'm asking you to make
an exception in this one case.

689
00:42:49,987 --> 00:42:51,655
You have no cash?

690
00:42:51,905 --> 00:42:53,824
- She has no cash.
- No, she has no cash.

691
00:42:54,074 --> 00:42:55,367
Get on another line, lady.

692
00:42:55,742 --> 00:42:57,035
I have a dollar.
That's all.

693
00:42:57,286 --> 00:42:58,996
I have a dollar. One dollar.

694
00:42:59,454 --> 00:43:01,206
Is there anything you can do?

695
00:43:03,000 --> 00:43:04,042
Need some money?

696
00:43:04,251 --> 00:43:06,920
I do not need any money.
Thank you very much.

697
00:43:07,171 --> 00:43:09,047
Get in another line.

698
00:43:09,715 --> 00:43:10,924
Rose.

699
00:43:11,300 --> 00:43:13,260
That is a great name. Rose.

700
00:43:13,677 --> 00:43:15,512
This is Kathleen.
I'm Joe...

701
00:43:15,762 --> 00:43:16,972
And I'm Henry.

702
00:43:17,222 --> 00:43:18,932
Henry, how are you?
Happy holidays.

703
00:43:19,183 --> 00:43:21,393
This is a credit card machine.

704
00:43:22,102 --> 00:43:23,103
Happy Thanksgiving.

705
00:43:24,730 --> 00:43:26,523
It's your turn to say
"Happy Thanksgiving" back.

706
00:43:27,399 --> 00:43:29,234
Happy Thanksgiving back.

707
00:43:29,693 --> 00:43:30,569
Knock, knock.

708
00:43:31,612 --> 00:43:32,529
Who's there?

709
00:43:33,071 --> 00:43:33,906
Orange.

710
00:43:34,740 --> 00:43:35,741
Orange who?

711
00:43:35,991 --> 00:43:37,409
Orange you going to
give us a break...

712
00:43:37,659 --> 00:43:41,121
...by zipping this credit card
through the machine?

713
00:43:41,997 --> 00:43:43,207
You can do it.

714
00:43:43,415 --> 00:43:44,208
Zip, zip.

715
00:43:45,167 --> 00:43:46,251
There you go.

716
00:43:47,252 --> 00:43:48,128
Rose?

717
00:43:50,172 --> 00:43:52,591
That is a great name.

718
00:43:53,091 --> 00:43:53,926
So you're fine.

719
00:43:55,093 --> 00:43:55,886
Fine.

720
00:43:56,136 --> 00:43:58,931
Happy Thanksgiving.
Henry, happy Thanksgiving.

721
00:44:00,182 --> 00:44:01,600
I'm so sorry.

722
00:44:01,850 --> 00:44:04,269
- From the bottom of my heart.
- So sign already!

723
00:44:04,978 --> 00:44:08,232
- I'd like to get home.
- You have my pen.

724
00:44:12,027 --> 00:44:15,197
The sun will come out tomorrow

725
00:44:15,614 --> 00:44:17,533
Bet your bottom dollar that

726
00:44:17,783 --> 00:44:20,619
Tomorrow there'll be sun

727
00:44:22,162 --> 00:44:24,915
Just thinking about tomorrow

728
00:44:25,958 --> 00:44:28,293
Clears away the cobwebs and the sorrow

729
00:44:29,461 --> 00:44:30,838
Till there's none

730
00:44:31,630 --> 00:44:34,258
When I'm stuck with a day that's gray

731
00:44:34,508 --> 00:44:35,801
And lonely

732
00:44:36,510 --> 00:44:39,263
I just stick out my chin and grin

733
00:44:39,513 --> 00:44:41,223
And say:

734
00:44:42,391 --> 00:44:45,143
Oh, the sun will come out tomorrow

735
00:44:45,394 --> 00:44:48,313
So you got to hang on till tomorrow

736
00:44:49,398 --> 00:44:51,191
Come what may

737
00:44:52,484 --> 00:44:54,862
Tomorrow, tomorrow

738
00:44:55,112 --> 00:44:57,447
I love you, tomorrow

739
00:44:57,698 --> 00:45:00,325
You're always a day

740
00:45:01,660 --> 00:45:03,036
Away

741
00:45:11,503 --> 00:45:12,254
Wonderful!

742
00:45:12,546 --> 00:45:13,380
Thank you.

743
00:45:13,630 --> 00:45:15,591
The violins

744
00:45:16,091 --> 00:45:19,261
Sing with joyful ring

745
00:45:20,095 --> 00:45:21,972
The violins

746
00:45:22,222 --> 00:45:25,684
Sing with joyful ring

747
00:45:27,519 --> 00:45:31,023
The clarinet, the clarinet

748
00:45:31,356 --> 00:45:34,067
Goes doodly, doodly,
doodly, doodly det

749
00:45:41,742 --> 00:45:43,827
The horn, the horn

750
00:45:45,787 --> 00:45:47,456
It sounds...

751
00:45:47,706 --> 00:45:50,334
The horn sounds so forlorn

752
00:45:56,048 --> 00:45:57,090
I got it.

753
00:46:53,772 --> 00:46:56,650
"It's coming on Christmas.
They're cutting down trees."

754
00:46:57,317 --> 00:46:59,486
Do you know that Joni Mitchell song?

755
00:46:59,736 --> 00:47:02,781
"I wish I had a river
I could skate away on."

756
00:47:03,031 --> 00:47:06,368
Such a sad song. And not
really about Christmas.

757
00:47:06,577 --> 00:47:10,622
I was thinking about it tonight
as I was decorating my tree.

758
00:47:10,873 --> 00:47:13,417
Unwrapping ornaments
made of Popsicle sticks...

759
00:47:13,667 --> 00:47:16,879
...and missing my mother so much
I almost couldn't breathe.

760
00:47:18,213 --> 00:47:20,215
I always miss my mother at Christmas.

761
00:47:20,465 --> 00:47:24,636
But it is worse this year
since I need some advice from her.

762
00:47:24,887 --> 00:47:26,263
I need her to make me cocoa.

763
00:47:26,513 --> 00:47:31,143
And tell me that everything going badly
in my life will sort itself out.

764
00:47:33,645 --> 00:47:36,690
What kind of advice do you need?
Can I help?

765
00:47:43,280 --> 00:47:44,823
Can you help?

766
00:47:46,867 --> 00:47:48,577
I wish you could help.

767
00:47:50,537 --> 00:47:51,413
I wish...

768
00:47:53,749 --> 00:47:56,001
I had a feeling
you'd be online now.

769
00:47:59,254 --> 00:48:00,297
I can give you advice.

770
00:48:00,547 --> 00:48:02,382
I'm great at advice.

771
00:48:12,851 --> 00:48:14,895
If only you could help.

772
00:48:19,358 --> 00:48:20,984
Is it about love?

773
00:48:21,235 --> 00:48:22,653
Please say no.

774
00:48:23,654 --> 00:48:25,656
No. How cute is that?

775
00:48:25,906 --> 00:48:28,075
My business is in trouble.

776
00:48:30,869 --> 00:48:32,120
Well!

777
00:48:35,749 --> 00:48:37,876
I'm a brilliant businessman.

778
00:48:38,293 --> 00:48:40,128
It's what I do best.

779
00:48:40,379 --> 00:48:42,005
What's your business?

780
00:48:44,633 --> 00:48:46,760
No specifics. Remember?

781
00:48:53,475 --> 00:48:57,980
Minus specifics, it's hard
to help except to say:

782
00:48:58,230 --> 00:48:59,648
"Go to the mattresses."

783
00:49:00,983 --> 00:49:03,277
Except to say, "Go to the matt..."

784
00:49:04,653 --> 00:49:05,612
What?

785
00:49:07,656 --> 00:49:08,574
What does that mean?

786
00:49:09,449 --> 00:49:13,078
It's from The Godfather.
It means you have to go to war.

787
00:49:15,789 --> 00:49:18,917
What is it with men
and The Godfather?

788
00:49:19,293 --> 00:49:20,294
Hello?

789
00:49:22,713 --> 00:49:24,631
Oh, come on. Hello?

790
00:49:25,340 --> 00:49:26,592
Well...

791
00:49:27,134 --> 00:49:29,052
Well, what can I...

792
00:49:29,386 --> 00:49:30,512
Michael...

793
00:49:31,138 --> 00:49:34,141
The Godfather is the I-Ching.

794
00:49:35,184 --> 00:49:37,352
The Godfather is the sum of all wisdom.

795
00:49:38,937 --> 00:49:41,523
The Godfather is the answer
to any question.

796
00:49:41,773 --> 00:49:43,859
What should I pack
for my vacation?

797
00:49:44,151 --> 00:49:46,153
"Leave the gun. Take the cannoli."

798
00:49:47,279 --> 00:49:49,364
What day of the week is it?

799
00:49:50,157 --> 00:49:53,660
"Monday, Tuesday,
Thursday, Wednesday."

800
00:49:54,453 --> 00:49:59,208
The answer to your question is:
"Go to the mattresses."

801
00:50:00,209 --> 00:50:02,753
You're at war.
"It's not personal, it's business.

802
00:50:03,003 --> 00:50:06,507
It's not personal.
It's business."

803
00:50:06,882 --> 00:50:11,845
Recite that to yourself every time
you're losing your nerve.

804
00:50:14,473 --> 00:50:15,974
I know you worry about being brave.

805
00:50:16,225 --> 00:50:17,309
Don't.

806
00:50:18,393 --> 00:50:20,229
This is your chance.

807
00:50:20,854 --> 00:50:22,189
Fight!

808
00:50:22,856 --> 00:50:24,066
Fight to the death!

809
00:50:30,864 --> 00:50:33,325
It's not personal.
It's business. Just fight!

810
00:50:33,575 --> 00:50:34,409
Fight!

811
00:50:44,127 --> 00:50:45,546
I've been thinking.

812
00:50:47,256 --> 00:50:48,257
What?

813
00:50:49,007 --> 00:50:50,884
I'm going to the mattresses.

814
00:50:51,093 --> 00:50:55,889
Would it be a conflict of interest
if you wrote about the store?

815
00:50:56,098 --> 00:50:56,932
Yes.

816
00:50:57,266 --> 00:50:58,100
Yes?

817
00:50:58,684 --> 00:50:59,685
No.

818
00:51:00,602 --> 00:51:02,062
So you'll do it?

819
00:51:05,190 --> 00:51:07,359
Do you know what it is
to go to the mattresses?

820
00:51:07,568 --> 00:51:09,069
From The Godfather.

821
00:51:12,990 --> 00:51:15,159
Good morning,
Shop Around The Corner.

822
00:51:15,367 --> 00:51:16,660
May I help you?

823
00:51:18,203 --> 00:51:20,122
The Channel 2 truck just pulled up.

824
00:51:20,372 --> 00:51:21,206
In a second.

825
00:51:21,456 --> 00:51:23,208
Everyone's read the article.

826
00:51:23,458 --> 00:51:26,712
"So you do not have to look
to the usual places...

827
00:51:26,962 --> 00:51:28,964
...where good and evil face off...

828
00:51:29,214 --> 00:51:33,427
...the places Herodotus called
'the happy land of absolutes."

829
00:51:33,969 --> 00:51:37,014
We have the perfect example
on the West Side...

830
00:51:37,264 --> 00:51:40,267
...where the cold cash cow,
Fox Books...

831
00:51:40,517 --> 00:51:42,561
...threatens survival of a temple...

832
00:51:42,811 --> 00:51:46,398
...to one of the twentieth century's
most profound truths:

833
00:51:46,607 --> 00:51:48,567
You are what you read."

834
00:51:48,942 --> 00:51:50,486
I believe that.

835
00:51:50,986 --> 00:51:52,863
"Save The Shop Around The Corner...

836
00:51:53,113 --> 00:51:55,657
...and you will save your soul."

837
00:51:57,534 --> 00:51:59,912
Frank, that's charming.

838
00:52:00,621 --> 00:52:01,830
Is it a little over the top?

839
00:52:02,080 --> 00:52:05,792
That was The Village Voice.
I told them to come over whenever.

840
00:52:07,753 --> 00:52:10,214
"Float like a butterfly.
Sting like a bee."

841
00:52:10,422 --> 00:52:11,507
One, two, three, four!

842
00:52:11,757 --> 00:52:13,717
We don't want your superstore!

843
00:52:13,967 --> 00:52:15,969
Five, six, seven, eight!

844
00:52:16,220 --> 00:52:18,347
Go away and close the gate!

845
00:52:18,597 --> 00:52:20,682
One, two, three, four!

846
00:52:20,933 --> 00:52:23,685
"We don't want this superstore."

847
00:52:23,936 --> 00:52:25,479
- Is that it?
- Catchy.

848
00:52:25,854 --> 00:52:27,064
Who wrote that?

849
00:52:27,272 --> 00:52:28,315
Annoying.

850
00:52:28,941 --> 00:52:30,526
Pissing me off.

851
00:52:30,776 --> 00:52:31,860
Do you want the West Side...

852
00:52:32,110 --> 00:52:35,280
...to become one big,
gigantic strip mall?

853
00:52:37,115 --> 00:52:40,410
Do you want to get off the subway
at 72nd and Broadway...

854
00:52:40,661 --> 00:52:43,121
...and not even know
you're in New York City?

855
00:52:44,039 --> 00:52:46,875
Can we save
The Shop Around The Corner?

856
00:52:48,502 --> 00:52:49,336
We're here...

857
00:52:49,586 --> 00:52:53,173
...at The Shop Around The Corner,
the West Side children 's bookstore...

858
00:52:53,423 --> 00:52:55,884
...on the verge of having
to close its doors...

859
00:52:56,134 --> 00:53:00,472
...because the big bad wolf,
Fox Books, has opened nearby...

860
00:53:00,722 --> 00:53:04,852
...wooing customers with its sharp
discounts and designer coffee.

861
00:53:06,061 --> 00:53:07,813
They have to have
discounts and lattes...

862
00:53:08,063 --> 00:53:10,190
...because their workers
have never read a book.

863
00:53:10,440 --> 00:53:13,360
She's not as nice as she seems on TV.

864
00:53:13,610 --> 00:53:14,987
You met her?

865
00:53:15,529 --> 00:53:17,197
Boy, she's a pill!

866
00:53:17,698 --> 00:53:20,075
Probably ain't as
fine as she look on TV.

867
00:53:20,659 --> 00:53:22,286
No, no. She's beautiful.

868
00:53:22,536 --> 00:53:23,370
But...

869
00:53:23,662 --> 00:53:25,164
...she's a pill.

870
00:53:25,497 --> 00:53:26,456
You don't feel bad...

871
00:53:26,707 --> 00:53:30,210
...about sending her ass back
to the projects with food stamps?

872
00:53:30,419 --> 00:53:31,962
Broke, single, white lady.

873
00:53:32,212 --> 00:53:33,255
It's not personal.

874
00:53:33,505 --> 00:53:34,423
It's business.

875
00:53:35,841 --> 00:53:37,968
Here's a good-looking guy.

876
00:53:38,218 --> 00:53:40,846
I sell cheap books.
So sue me.

877
00:53:41,388 --> 00:53:44,016
That, in a nutshell,
is the Fox Books philosophy.

878
00:53:45,100 --> 00:53:45,934
That's what you said?

879
00:53:46,185 --> 00:53:47,644
Discount them and sell them.

880
00:53:47,895 --> 00:53:49,354
That's not all I said!

881
00:53:49,605 --> 00:53:52,274
I can't believe those bastards.
I said we were great!

882
00:53:52,524 --> 00:53:54,943
I said you could read for hours
and no one will bother you.

883
00:53:55,194 --> 00:53:58,322
I said we had 150,000 titles.

884
00:53:58,572 --> 00:54:00,699
I said we were a goddamn piazza.

885
00:54:00,949 --> 00:54:03,327
A place where people could mingle.

886
00:54:03,577 --> 00:54:04,453
Piazza?

887
00:54:04,703 --> 00:54:06,330
I was eloquent!

888
00:54:06,705 --> 00:54:07,789
Shit!

889
00:54:09,124 --> 00:54:12,669
It's inevitable. People want
to turn her into Joan of Arc.

890
00:54:12,920 --> 00:54:14,671
And you into Attila the Hun.

891
00:54:14,922 --> 00:54:17,799
Not me personally,
but the company, yeah.

892
00:54:19,051 --> 00:54:20,469
I have met Joe Fox.

893
00:54:21,136 --> 00:54:24,681
I've heard him compare
his store to a Price Club...

894
00:54:24,932 --> 00:54:27,392
...and the books in it
to cans of olive oil.

895
00:54:31,438 --> 00:54:34,483
The bookstore, tell us about it.

896
00:54:36,026 --> 00:54:41,114
The Shop Around The Corner has
a kind of Jeffersonian purity to it...

897
00:54:41,365 --> 00:54:46,036
...that the city needs in order
to maintain historical integrity.

898
00:54:46,286 --> 00:54:47,371
Jeffersonian purity.

899
00:54:47,621 --> 00:54:49,665
That was nice. Thank you.

900
00:54:49,998 --> 00:54:51,917
- Are you taping this?
- I'm taping this.

901
00:54:52,125 --> 00:54:54,711
Technologically speaking,
the world's out of hand.

902
00:54:54,962 --> 00:54:56,588
Take the VCR.

903
00:54:56,922 --> 00:55:01,176
The whole idea behind the VCR
is that it makes it possible...

904
00:55:01,426 --> 00:55:04,930
...for you to tape what's on TV
when you leave the house.

905
00:55:05,180 --> 00:55:07,599
The whole idea
behind leaving the house...

906
00:55:07,850 --> 00:55:11,395
...is so you can miss
what's on television.

907
00:55:11,645 --> 00:55:13,438
- I've heard you say that before.
- She hasn't.

908
00:55:13,689 --> 00:55:14,940
Absolutely!

909
00:55:15,190 --> 00:55:16,608
Right!

910
00:55:16,984 --> 00:55:18,402
She gets it.

911
00:55:18,652 --> 00:55:20,612
Radio. There's a medium
I can get behind.

912
00:55:21,947 --> 00:55:23,615
Will you start collecting radios?

913
00:55:23,866 --> 00:55:25,033
We're on television.

914
00:55:25,826 --> 00:55:27,661
You're good at it.

915
00:55:30,622 --> 00:55:32,416
She's coming on to you.

916
00:55:32,666 --> 00:55:34,585
They do this on television.

917
00:55:34,835 --> 00:55:36,003
The Shop Around The Corner...

918
00:55:36,253 --> 00:55:38,755
...it's a true New York treasure.

919
00:55:39,006 --> 00:55:40,257
As are you.

920
00:55:43,886 --> 00:55:46,054
Honestly, I'd love to have you back.

921
00:55:46,388 --> 00:55:47,806
Is she sweating?

922
00:55:48,140 --> 00:55:50,642
She's touching herself
And she's sweating.

923
00:55:50,893 --> 00:55:51,894
Anytime.

924
00:55:54,104 --> 00:55:55,397
We can turn it off.

925
00:55:55,647 --> 00:55:56,940
Oh, my God.

926
00:55:57,191 --> 00:56:00,360
I just want to say that
yours is the only show I watch.

927
00:56:00,861 --> 00:56:01,904
Oh, my God!

928
00:56:02,196 --> 00:56:04,114
No! I was being polite.

929
00:56:04,364 --> 00:56:05,741
Thank you, Frank Navasky.

930
00:56:06,533 --> 00:56:08,202
Thank you're...?
I'm sorry.

931
00:56:08,452 --> 00:56:09,620
Thank you're?

932
00:56:09,995 --> 00:56:11,538
Thank you're,
ladies and gentlemen.

933
00:56:12,831 --> 00:56:15,083
I slobbered all over her, didn't I?

934
00:56:16,168 --> 00:56:19,588
But I think that
there's something there.

935
00:56:31,350 --> 00:56:35,354
So first, I got to go get
some eucalyptus candles...

936
00:56:35,604 --> 00:56:39,066
...because it makes
my apartment smell mossy.

937
00:56:39,608 --> 00:56:42,694
Then I'm going to the market,
and I can't decide whether to get...

938
00:56:42,945 --> 00:56:45,948
...sausage for the meat sauce,
or chopped meat.

939
00:56:46,365 --> 00:56:47,783
Spare us.

940
00:56:48,075 --> 00:56:49,368
You know what? Clam sauce.

941
00:56:49,993 --> 00:56:51,411
Because this is a big date.

942
00:56:56,875 --> 00:56:59,336
Don't tell me.
Not the slightest difference?

943
00:57:01,129 --> 00:57:02,840
How can that be?

944
00:57:03,090 --> 00:57:06,552
All this publicity
and not one bit of difference?

945
00:57:08,136 --> 00:57:10,556
Birdie, what am I going to do?

946
00:57:11,098 --> 00:57:12,349
What would Mom have done?

947
00:57:14,101 --> 00:57:16,645
Well, let's ask her.

948
00:57:20,524 --> 00:57:23,569
Cecilia, what should we do?

949
00:57:28,031 --> 00:57:29,867
She has no idea.

950
00:57:30,117 --> 00:57:33,579
But she thinks
the window display is lovely.

951
00:57:38,667 --> 00:57:40,294
Good night, dearie.

952
00:57:55,684 --> 00:57:56,852
I need help.

953
00:57:59,980 --> 00:58:01,607
Do you still want to meet me?

954
00:58:04,776 --> 00:58:07,654
I would love to meet you.
Where? When?

955
00:58:08,113 --> 00:58:12,117
So I suppose she's carrying a copy
of a book with a flower in it?

956
00:58:14,119 --> 00:58:15,287
Not really?

957
00:58:15,829 --> 00:58:17,998
She could be a real dog, man.

958
00:58:18,248 --> 00:58:20,042
I'm only staying 10 minutes.

959
00:58:20,501 --> 00:58:24,004
I'll say hello, have a cup
of coffee and then I'll split.

960
00:58:24,755 --> 00:58:28,258
Hope she doesn't have a squeaky voice
like the mice in Cinderella.

961
00:58:28,509 --> 00:58:32,012
Why am I doing this?
Why am I compelled to meet her?

962
00:58:33,263 --> 00:58:35,933
Relax. You're taking it
to the next level.

963
00:58:36,183 --> 00:58:37,434
I always do that.

964
00:58:37,684 --> 00:58:39,812
I always take a relationship
to the next level.

965
00:58:40,062 --> 00:58:43,649
If that works out, I take it
to the next level after that.

966
00:58:43,899 --> 00:58:47,986
Till I reach that level where
it becomes absolutely necessary...

967
00:58:48,237 --> 00:58:49,196
...for me to leave.

968
00:58:51,573 --> 00:58:54,368
I won't stay that long anyway.
I already said that, didn't I?

969
00:58:54,576 --> 00:58:56,203
Yes, you did.

970
00:59:00,165 --> 00:59:03,460
Café Lalo. This is it.

971
00:59:04,545 --> 00:59:07,256
Eight o'clock.
We got here fast, didn't we?

972
00:59:07,673 --> 00:59:08,924
Kevin...

973
00:59:10,092 --> 00:59:13,262
...this woman is the most
adorable creature.

974
00:59:13,512 --> 00:59:17,307
If she turns out even to be
as good-looking as a mailbox...

975
00:59:17,558 --> 00:59:21,395
...I'd be crazy not to turn my life
upside down and marry her.

976
00:59:22,104 --> 00:59:24,106
She could be a real dog.

977
00:59:24,356 --> 00:59:25,440
But good luck.

978
00:59:29,987 --> 00:59:31,864
Would you go and look for me?

979
00:59:32,114 --> 00:59:35,242
Go look through the window
and check her out. Please?

980
00:59:35,492 --> 00:59:36,535
All right.

981
00:59:38,328 --> 00:59:40,414
You're pathetic, man.

982
00:59:43,417 --> 00:59:45,252
I'll see what I see.

983
00:59:46,128 --> 00:59:47,337
You see her?

984
00:59:48,630 --> 00:59:49,631
Wait, yeah.

985
00:59:50,174 --> 00:59:52,384
I see a very beautiful girl.

986
00:59:52,968 --> 00:59:54,386
She's fine.

987
00:59:55,429 --> 00:59:57,139
- I knew it.
- She's gorgeous.

988
00:59:57,389 --> 00:59:58,849
I knew she would be.

989
00:59:59,099 --> 01:00:00,559
But no book.

990
01:00:04,021 --> 01:00:04,855
All right.

991
01:00:05,105 --> 01:00:07,107
Okay, wait.
Wait a minute.

992
01:00:07,357 --> 01:00:10,527
There's a book with a flower.
So this got to be her.

993
01:00:10,777 --> 01:00:12,237
What does she look like?

994
01:00:12,821 --> 01:00:14,072
Can't see. Waiter's blocking.

995
01:00:14,364 --> 01:00:15,574
Damn it!

996
01:00:15,824 --> 01:00:16,909
He's moving.

997
01:00:20,162 --> 01:00:21,246
Can you see her?

998
01:00:23,123 --> 01:00:23,999
Can you see her?

999
01:00:25,959 --> 01:00:27,085
Yeah.

1000
01:00:27,336 --> 01:00:28,212
And?

1001
01:00:30,756 --> 01:00:32,216
She's very pretty.

1002
01:00:32,466 --> 01:00:33,842
She is!

1003
01:00:34,092 --> 01:00:36,512
I knew she would be!
She had to be!

1004
01:00:36,762 --> 01:00:38,639
She had to be!

1005
01:00:40,724 --> 01:00:41,892
You know what?

1006
01:00:42,100 --> 01:00:43,310
She look...

1007
01:00:44,520 --> 01:00:47,940
I mean, she almost has
the same coloring as...

1008
01:00:48,482 --> 01:00:49,691
...that Kathleen Kelly person.

1009
01:00:49,942 --> 01:00:51,193
Kathleen Kelly of the bookstore?

1010
01:00:51,443 --> 01:00:53,070
Why not? You said you thought
she was attractive.

1011
01:00:53,320 --> 01:00:56,532
Absolutely. Why not?
Who cares about Kathleen Kelly?

1012
01:00:56,782 --> 01:01:00,118
Well, if you don't like
Kathleen Kelly...

1013
01:01:00,452 --> 01:01:02,329
...I can tell you right now...

1014
01:01:03,080 --> 01:01:04,164
...you won't like this girl.

1015
01:01:05,582 --> 01:01:06,416
Why not?

1016
01:01:08,377 --> 01:01:10,170
Because it is Kathleen Kelly.

1017
01:01:23,517 --> 01:01:24,977
What are you going to do?

1018
01:01:28,939 --> 01:01:30,315
Nothing.

1019
01:01:31,108 --> 01:01:32,860
You're going to let her
wait all night?

1020
01:01:33,110 --> 01:01:35,279
That's exactly
what I'm going to do.

1021
01:01:36,613 --> 01:01:38,991
Good night.
I'll see you in the morning.

1022
01:01:48,125 --> 01:01:49,543
Do you mind if I borrow this chair?

1023
01:01:49,793 --> 01:01:51,587
Yes, I mind. Sorry.

1024
01:01:51,837 --> 01:01:53,672
I'm expecting someone.

1025
01:02:07,686 --> 01:02:09,730
Would you like another tea?

1026
01:02:10,147 --> 01:02:11,648
Yes. Thank you.

1027
01:02:47,267 --> 01:02:50,187
This is a coincidence.
Would you mind if I sat down?

1028
01:02:50,437 --> 01:02:53,524
Yes, I would, actually.
I'm expecting someone.

1029
01:02:54,733 --> 01:02:55,567
Pride and Prejudice.

1030
01:02:56,610 --> 01:02:57,528
Do you mind?

1031
01:02:57,778 --> 01:03:00,864
I bet you read that book every year.
I bet you just love that...

1032
01:03:01,114 --> 01:03:02,783
...Mr. Darcy.

1033
01:03:03,033 --> 01:03:06,119
Your sentimental heart beats wildly
at the thought he and...

1034
01:03:06,411 --> 01:03:11,208
...whatever her name is are truly,
honestly going to end up together.

1035
01:03:15,045 --> 01:03:15,879
Can I get you something?

1036
01:03:16,129 --> 01:03:17,172
He's not staying.

1037
01:03:17,422 --> 01:03:19,007
Mochaccino decaf, nonfat.

1038
01:03:19,258 --> 01:03:21,218
You are not staying!

1039
01:03:21,426 --> 01:03:23,387
I'll stay until
your friend gets here.

1040
01:03:24,930 --> 01:03:25,973
Is he late?

1041
01:03:26,348 --> 01:03:29,518
The heroine of Pride and Prejudice
is Elizabeth Bennet.

1042
01:03:29,768 --> 01:03:33,272
She is a great and complex character.
Not that you would know.

1043
01:03:33,522 --> 01:03:35,357
As a matter of fact, I've read it.

1044
01:03:35,607 --> 01:03:36,817
Good for you.

1045
01:03:37,568 --> 01:03:39,778
You'd discover a lot
if you really knew me.

1046
01:03:40,362 --> 01:03:42,030
I know what I'd find.

1047
01:03:42,281 --> 01:03:46,285
Instead of a brain, a cash register.
Instead of a heart, a bottom line.

1048
01:03:50,205 --> 01:03:50,998
What?

1049
01:03:51,248 --> 01:03:53,458
- I just had a breakthrough.
- What is it?

1050
01:03:53,709 --> 01:03:58,630
For the first time, when confronted
with a horrible person...

1051
01:03:58,881 --> 01:04:01,341
...I knew what I wanted to say,
and I said it.

1052
01:04:02,009 --> 01:04:05,721
You have a gift for it. That's a
perfect blend of poetry and meanness.

1053
01:04:06,221 --> 01:04:07,931
Let me tell you about meanness.

1054
01:04:08,182 --> 01:04:11,768
Don't misunderstand.
I'm paying you a compliment.

1055
01:04:12,478 --> 01:04:13,896
What are you doing?

1056
01:04:14,104 --> 01:04:17,191
Is this a red rose?
No, it's a crimson rose.

1057
01:04:17,441 --> 01:04:18,942
Something you read in a book.

1058
01:04:19,193 --> 01:04:23,238
It's funny to you, isn't it?
Everything is a joke to you.

1059
01:04:32,915 --> 01:04:33,999
Please leave.

1060
01:04:34,374 --> 01:04:36,001
Please leave, I beg you.

1061
01:04:43,717 --> 01:04:44,676
Thank you.

1062
01:05:06,406 --> 01:05:09,451
That hanky reminds me
of the first day I met you.

1063
01:05:09,701 --> 01:05:11,495
First day you lied to me.

1064
01:05:11,870 --> 01:05:13,497
- I didn't lie.
- You did too.

1065
01:05:13,914 --> 01:05:15,874
- No, I didn't.
- Yes, you did.

1066
01:05:17,835 --> 01:05:18,836
You did too.

1067
01:05:19,086 --> 01:05:22,214
I thought that Fox stuff
was so charming. F-O-X.

1068
01:05:22,548 --> 01:05:23,841
I didn't lie about it.

1069
01:05:25,259 --> 01:05:26,844
"Just call me Joe."

1070
01:05:27,094 --> 01:05:27,928
Sure.

1071
01:05:28,220 --> 01:05:31,140
As if you were a stupid
22-year-old girl with no last name.

1072
01:05:31,390 --> 01:05:33,475
"Hi, I'm Kimberly."
"Hi, I'm Janice."

1073
01:05:33,725 --> 01:05:38,272
It's like they're an entire generation
of cocktail waitresses.

1074
01:05:38,522 --> 01:05:39,523
Look.

1075
01:05:41,567 --> 01:05:45,362
- I am not a cocktail waitress.
- That's not what I meant.

1076
01:05:45,571 --> 01:05:48,866
What I said about Price Club,
that's not what I meant.

1077
01:05:49,366 --> 01:05:53,579
You poor, sad multimillionaire.
I feel so sorry for you.

1078
01:05:59,626 --> 01:06:02,546
I'll take a wild guess
that's not him, either.

1079
01:06:03,964 --> 01:06:06,466
So who is he, I wonder?

1080
01:06:06,675 --> 01:06:09,178
Certainly not
the world's greatest expert...

1081
01:06:09,428 --> 01:06:11,471
...on Julius and Ethel Rosenberg.

1082
01:06:12,139 --> 01:06:14,516
But somebody else entirely different.

1083
01:06:15,267 --> 01:06:17,144
Will you be mean to him too?

1084
01:06:17,436 --> 01:06:18,812
No, I will not.

1085
01:06:19,313 --> 01:06:22,524
The man who is coming here
is completely unlike you.

1086
01:06:22,774 --> 01:06:25,819
The man who is coming here
is kind and funny.

1087
01:06:26,069 --> 01:06:28,322
He's got a wonderful sense of humor.

1088
01:06:28,572 --> 01:06:29,573
But...

1089
01:06:30,324 --> 01:06:31,742
...he's not here.

1090
01:06:32,826 --> 01:06:34,703
If he's not, he has a reason...

1091
01:06:34,953 --> 01:06:38,290
...because there is not a cruel
or careless bone in his body.

1092
01:06:38,499 --> 01:06:40,667
I wouldn't expect you to understand.

1093
01:06:40,918 --> 01:06:45,839
You, with your theme park,
homogenize-the-world mochaccinoland.

1094
01:06:46,089 --> 01:06:50,636
You think you're a benefactor
bringing books to the masses.

1095
01:06:50,886 --> 01:06:53,514
But no one will ever remember you.

1096
01:06:53,806 --> 01:06:56,141
And maybe no one will remember me.

1097
01:06:56,350 --> 01:06:58,435
But plenty of people
remember my mother.

1098
01:06:58,685 --> 01:06:59,895
They think she was fine.

1099
01:07:00,145 --> 01:07:02,773
They think her store
was something special.

1100
01:07:04,191 --> 01:07:06,693
You are nothing but a suit.

1101
01:07:20,374 --> 01:07:21,875
That's my cue.

1102
01:07:25,587 --> 01:07:26,421
Good night.

1103
01:08:42,206 --> 01:08:46,168
So then the agent asks for $600,000
and I said to her:

1104
01:08:46,418 --> 01:08:49,004
"If you think I'm
even going to talk...

1105
01:08:49,213 --> 01:08:51,965
...about paying that kind of
advance for an author...

1106
01:08:52,174 --> 01:08:56,678
...whose last book is being used
as trivets all over the world...

1107
01:08:56,929 --> 01:08:59,181
...you are completely crazy."

1108
01:08:59,556 --> 01:09:03,227
She was insulting, and
the only thing pleasant about her...

1109
01:09:04,144 --> 01:09:07,064
...was the way her hair
fell across her forehead.

1110
01:09:07,272 --> 01:09:11,151
Underneath that disagreeable
exterior, she may be...

1111
01:09:11,401 --> 01:09:13,278
A real bitch!

1112
01:09:13,612 --> 01:09:17,533
Let's not talk about it.
I'm sure you have work to do.

1113
01:09:17,783 --> 01:09:18,742
Not really.

1114
01:09:18,992 --> 01:09:22,496
This place is a well-oiled
machine, my friend.

1115
01:09:24,164 --> 01:09:26,416
So? What happened?

1116
01:09:26,959 --> 01:09:29,545
- He never came.
- He stood you up.

1117
01:09:30,045 --> 01:09:33,423
I wouldn't characterize it that way.
I think something happened.

1118
01:09:33,632 --> 01:09:37,511
Something unexpected that
made it impossible for him to...

1119
01:09:38,136 --> 01:09:41,098
What if he showed up,
took one look at me and left?

1120
01:09:41,306 --> 01:09:43,183
Not possible.

1121
01:09:43,934 --> 01:09:46,812
Maybe there was a subway accident.

1122
01:09:47,187 --> 01:09:48,188
Absolutely!

1123
01:09:48,397 --> 01:09:50,691
A train got trapped underground
with him inside.

1124
01:09:50,983 --> 01:09:51,692
And no phone.

1125
01:09:51,942 --> 01:09:54,403
And you know how those
express trains create suction.

1126
01:09:54,653 --> 01:09:56,488
He got sucked onto the tracks.

1127
01:09:56,780 --> 01:09:57,698
The third rail.

1128
01:09:58,115 --> 01:09:59,700
He's toast!

1129
01:10:00,033 --> 01:10:00,868
What happened?

1130
01:10:02,286 --> 01:10:03,537
He was unable to make it.

1131
01:10:04,288 --> 01:10:05,497
He stood you up?

1132
01:10:08,625 --> 01:10:13,172
Maybe he had a car accident.
Those cab drivers are maniacs.

1133
01:10:13,422 --> 01:10:17,467
They hit something and you slam
into that plastic partition.

1134
01:10:17,718 --> 01:10:21,096
Or his elbows could be in splints,
so he couldn't dial.

1135
01:10:21,346 --> 01:10:23,640
Or he could be unconscious.

1136
01:10:23,891 --> 01:10:24,850
In a coma.

1137
01:10:25,476 --> 01:10:27,311
Stuck in intensive care.

1138
01:10:27,603 --> 01:10:29,146
With a heart monitor beeping.

1139
01:10:29,396 --> 01:10:30,522
And like...

1140
01:10:30,772 --> 01:10:31,857
No phone.

1141
01:10:40,240 --> 01:10:41,116
What are you saying?

1142
01:10:42,659 --> 01:10:43,494
It could be.

1143
01:10:44,077 --> 01:10:46,371
He was arrested near the café.

1144
01:10:46,622 --> 01:10:48,373
Is there a picture?

1145
01:10:51,793 --> 01:10:52,836
That explains it.

1146
01:10:54,087 --> 01:10:54,880
He was in jail.

1147
01:10:55,714 --> 01:10:57,591
And there was a phone.

1148
01:10:57,799 --> 01:10:59,968
He only got one call,
so he called his lawyer.

1149
01:11:00,219 --> 01:11:01,595
You are so lucky!

1150
01:11:01,803 --> 01:11:03,430
You could be dead.

1151
01:11:06,058 --> 01:11:08,769
He couldn't be the Rooftop Killer!

1152
01:11:09,019 --> 01:11:12,314
Remember when you thought
Frank might be the Unabomber?

1153
01:11:14,191 --> 01:11:15,567
That was different.

1154
01:11:16,360 --> 01:11:18,654
How long did you sit there alone?

1155
01:11:18,904 --> 01:11:21,240
Not long. Joe Fox came in.

1156
01:11:22,449 --> 01:11:25,160
I don't want to talk about it.
Let's get to work.

1157
01:11:25,369 --> 01:11:28,205
There's got to be something to do.

1158
01:11:29,373 --> 01:11:32,543
- Look at this.
- He looks kind of cute.

1159
01:11:34,878 --> 01:11:35,796
So?

1160
01:11:36,255 --> 01:11:38,882
He was unavoidably detained.

1161
01:11:39,299 --> 01:11:41,510
He stood you up?

1162
01:11:45,556 --> 01:11:47,808
I've been thinking about you.

1163
01:11:48,725 --> 01:11:52,229
Last night I went to meet you,
and you weren't there.

1164
01:11:53,939 --> 01:11:57,568
I wish I knew why.
I felt so foolish.

1165
01:11:59,444 --> 01:12:01,280
As I waited, someone else showed up.

1166
01:12:01,530 --> 01:12:04,783
A man who has made
my professional life a misery.

1167
01:12:05,033 --> 01:12:07,452
And an amazing thing happened.

1168
01:12:09,913 --> 01:12:11,582
I was able, for the first time...

1169
01:12:11,832 --> 01:12:13,959
...to say the exact thing
I wanted to say...

1170
01:12:14,209 --> 01:12:17,129
...at the exact moment
I wanted to say it.

1171
01:12:17,588 --> 01:12:21,383
And of course,
afterwards I felt terrible...

1172
01:12:21,758 --> 01:12:23,969
...just as you said I would.

1173
01:12:24,970 --> 01:12:27,723
I was cruel, and I'm never cruel.

1174
01:12:28,557 --> 01:12:31,518
Though I hardly believe
what I said mattered to this man.

1175
01:12:31,768 --> 01:12:34,855
To him, I am just a bug to be crushed.

1176
01:12:35,606 --> 01:12:36,857
But what if it did?

1177
01:12:37,107 --> 01:12:41,111
No matter what he's done to me,
there is no excuse for my behavior.

1178
01:12:41,820 --> 01:12:43,197
Anyway...

1179
01:12:43,447 --> 01:12:45,866
...I so wanted to talk to you.

1180
01:12:46,700 --> 01:12:49,161
I hope you have a reason
for not coming.

1181
01:12:49,411 --> 01:12:53,332
You don 't seem like the kind of
person who'd do that.

1182
01:12:53,957 --> 01:12:55,918
The thing about this
form of communication...

1183
01:12:56,126 --> 01:12:58,921
...is you're likely to talk
about nothing.

1184
01:12:59,129 --> 01:13:00,839
But I just want to say...

1185
01:13:01,048 --> 01:13:04,676
...all this nothing has meant
more to me than so many...

1186
01:13:04,885 --> 01:13:06,512
...somethings.

1187
01:13:07,846 --> 01:13:09,640
So, thanks.

1188
01:13:26,114 --> 01:13:27,366
Goodbye.

1189
01:14:21,295 --> 01:14:23,005
I am in...

1190
01:14:26,425 --> 01:14:27,968
...Vancouver.

1191
01:14:37,728 --> 01:14:40,898
I was stuck in a meeting
which I couldn't get out of...

1192
01:14:41,106 --> 01:14:42,316
...and...

1193
01:14:42,649 --> 01:14:44,443
...the electricity went out.

1194
01:14:44,693 --> 01:14:46,028
And...

1195
01:14:46,403 --> 01:14:47,571
...we were trapped...

1196
01:14:47,821 --> 01:14:50,032
...on the 38th floor.

1197
01:14:51,700 --> 01:14:54,203
And the telephone system blew too.

1198
01:15:01,376 --> 01:15:03,212
Amazingly enough.

1199
01:15:38,330 --> 01:15:39,790
Dear friend:

1200
01:15:43,085 --> 01:15:46,004
I cannot tell you
what happened last night.

1201
01:15:47,256 --> 01:15:49,758
But I beg you
from the bottom of my heart...

1202
01:15:49,967 --> 01:15:52,052
...to forgive me for not being there.

1203
01:16:00,519 --> 01:16:02,104
For what happened.

1204
01:16:04,731 --> 01:16:08,235
I feel terrible that you
found yourself in a situation...

1205
01:16:08,485 --> 01:16:11,363
...that caused you additional pain.

1206
01:16:13,615 --> 01:16:15,576
But I'm absolutely sure...

1207
01:16:15,909 --> 01:16:19,454
...that whatever you said
last night was provoked.

1208
01:16:19,705 --> 01:16:21,373
Even deserved.

1209
01:16:24,877 --> 01:16:29,131
Everyone says things they regret
when they're stressed.

1210
01:16:31,175 --> 01:16:34,553
You were expecting to see
someone you trusted...

1211
01:16:34,761 --> 01:16:37,181
...and met the enemy instead.

1212
01:16:40,809 --> 01:16:42,686
The fault is mine.

1213
01:16:45,189 --> 01:16:47,858
Someday I'll explain everything.

1214
01:16:48,400 --> 01:16:49,651
Meanwhile...

1215
01:16:49,902 --> 01:16:51,278
...I'm still here.

1216
01:16:52,362 --> 01:16:53,363
Talk to me.

1217
01:16:53,572 --> 01:16:56,241
Did he say he wanted
to meet you again?

1218
01:16:56,492 --> 01:16:59,453
No, not really.
It doesn't matter.

1219
01:16:59,661 --> 01:17:02,748
We'll be like George Bernard Shaw
and Mrs. Campbell.

1220
01:17:02,998 --> 01:17:04,708
We'll write letters our whole lives.

1221
01:17:06,460 --> 01:17:09,213
Thank you for the scones.
They look lovely.

1222
01:17:09,463 --> 01:17:12,758
Birdie, where was this one taken?

1223
01:17:13,217 --> 01:17:14,176
Seville.

1224
01:17:14,593 --> 01:17:16,470
When you fell madly in love?

1225
01:17:17,638 --> 01:17:20,516
So, dearie,
what have you decided to do?

1226
01:17:20,766 --> 01:17:23,268
Close. We're going to close.

1227
01:17:24,311 --> 01:17:25,145
Close.

1228
01:17:25,896 --> 01:17:29,858
Closing the store is
the brave thing to do.

1229
01:17:30,984 --> 01:17:32,569
You are such a liar.

1230
01:17:32,820 --> 01:17:33,612
But thank you.

1231
01:17:34,947 --> 01:17:39,993
You are daring to imagine that
you could have a different life.

1232
01:17:40,786 --> 01:17:45,707
I know it doesn't feel like that.
You feel like a big failure.

1233
01:17:45,958 --> 01:17:47,125
But you're not.

1234
01:17:47,501 --> 01:17:51,630
You are marching into
the unknown, armed with...

1235
01:17:51,880 --> 01:17:52,840
...nothing.

1236
01:17:53,549 --> 01:17:55,175
Have a sandwich.

1237
01:17:56,385 --> 01:17:57,302
Not nothing.

1238
01:17:57,678 --> 01:17:59,638
I have a little money saved.

1239
01:17:59,972 --> 01:18:03,142
If you need more, ask me.
I'm very rich.

1240
01:18:03,392 --> 01:18:06,812
I bought Intel at six.

1241
01:18:10,190 --> 01:18:12,901
I suppose you want me
to tell you who it was...

1242
01:18:13,152 --> 01:18:15,487
...I fell madly in love with.

1243
01:18:16,405 --> 01:18:17,656
I'm not going to tell.

1244
01:18:18,532 --> 01:18:19,867
Who was it?

1245
01:18:20,826 --> 01:18:23,787
Come on, tell. That's so mean.

1246
01:18:24,955 --> 01:18:26,165
But so romantic.

1247
01:18:26,415 --> 01:18:28,834
- It wasn't meant to be.
- Why not?

1248
01:18:30,085 --> 01:18:31,628
He ran Spain.

1249
01:18:32,754 --> 01:18:33,589
Spain?

1250
01:18:34,464 --> 01:18:36,967
The country, he ran it.
It was his job.

1251
01:18:37,259 --> 01:18:38,969
And then he died.

1252
01:18:39,261 --> 01:18:40,888
Just as well.

1253
01:18:41,221 --> 01:18:42,598
Milk or lemon?

1254
01:18:44,183 --> 01:18:47,186
She fell in love with
Generalissimo Franco.

1255
01:18:47,478 --> 01:18:49,438
We don't know that for sure.

1256
01:18:49,730 --> 01:18:52,733
Who else could it have been?
It was around 1960.

1257
01:18:52,983 --> 01:18:55,569
- Do you want popcorn?
- I can't believe this!

1258
01:18:56,820 --> 01:19:01,366
It's not like he was something normal,
like a socialist or an anarchist.

1259
01:19:01,658 --> 01:19:03,202
It happened in Spain.

1260
01:19:03,452 --> 01:19:05,662
People do stupid things
in foreign countries.

1261
01:19:05,954 --> 01:19:08,957
They buy leather jackets
for more than they're worth.

1262
01:19:09,249 --> 01:19:12,503
But they don't fall in love
with fascist dictators.

1263
01:19:18,675 --> 01:19:21,512
Birdie is a very
special person to me.

1264
01:19:21,804 --> 01:19:24,473
She is practically my surrogate mother.

1265
01:19:24,681 --> 01:19:27,351
- She's out of her mind.
- She is not!

1266
01:19:27,768 --> 01:19:31,897
I could never be with anybody
who doesn't take politics seriously.

1267
01:19:32,105 --> 01:19:33,190
Do you mind?

1268
01:19:33,440 --> 01:19:34,900
A hot dog is singing.

1269
01:19:35,150 --> 01:19:38,153
You need quiet
while a hot dog is singing?

1270
01:19:41,615 --> 01:19:43,992
I have something to tell you.

1271
01:19:45,160 --> 01:19:46,245
I didn't vote.

1272
01:19:46,537 --> 01:19:47,246
What?

1273
01:19:47,621 --> 01:19:49,331
In the last mayoral election...

1274
01:19:49,623 --> 01:19:52,042
...when Rudy Giuliani was running
against Ruth Messinger...

1275
01:19:52,876 --> 01:19:54,586
...I went to get a manicure.

1276
01:19:54,837 --> 01:19:56,839
And forgot to vote.

1277
01:19:57,297 --> 01:19:58,674
Since when do you get manicures?

1278
01:19:58,882 --> 01:20:01,969
I suppose you could never be with
a woman who got manicures?

1279
01:20:03,095 --> 01:20:04,972
It's okay. I forgive you.

1280
01:20:06,640 --> 01:20:07,641
You forgive me?

1281
01:20:16,984 --> 01:20:19,570
Excuse me, I'm sorry. Excuse me.

1282
01:20:20,737 --> 01:20:22,739
This has been a big week.
You're closing the store.

1283
01:20:22,990 --> 01:20:26,201
It's not that.
Really, it's not.

1284
01:20:26,493 --> 01:20:29,413
I know, I know.
That was terrible of me.

1285
01:20:30,914 --> 01:20:31,874
What was terrible?

1286
01:20:32,166 --> 01:20:35,002
Jumping all over you
when I'm the one...

1287
01:20:36,545 --> 01:20:38,964
I don't even know how to say this.

1288
01:20:40,174 --> 01:20:41,425
What is it?

1289
01:20:42,509 --> 01:20:43,385
What?

1290
01:20:45,220 --> 01:20:47,514
You're a wonderful person.

1291
01:20:48,056 --> 01:20:49,683
So are you.

1292
01:20:49,933 --> 01:20:54,062
And I'm so honored that
you'd want to be with me...

1293
01:20:54,354 --> 01:20:58,025
...because you wouldn't be with
anyone who wasn't truly worthy.

1294
01:20:58,567 --> 01:21:01,320
I feel exactly the same way.

1295
01:21:01,778 --> 01:21:05,073
Don't say that.
That makes it worse.

1296
01:21:05,407 --> 01:21:06,283
What?

1297
01:21:08,494 --> 01:21:09,495
You don't love me.

1298
01:21:16,710 --> 01:21:17,544
Me either.

1299
01:21:18,712 --> 01:21:19,671
You don't love me?

1300
01:21:22,174 --> 01:21:23,217
No.

1301
01:21:23,592 --> 01:21:25,427
But we're so right for each other.

1302
01:21:25,719 --> 01:21:27,554
I know!

1303
01:21:30,557 --> 01:21:32,893
Well, is there someone else?

1304
01:21:34,144 --> 01:21:36,438
That woman on television, Sidney Ann.

1305
01:21:38,273 --> 01:21:40,192
Nothing has happened, but...

1306
01:21:41,610 --> 01:21:43,153
Is she a Republican?

1307
01:21:43,529 --> 01:21:46,073
I can't help myself.

1308
01:21:55,249 --> 01:21:56,375
What about you?

1309
01:21:56,959 --> 01:21:58,669
Is there someone else?

1310
01:22:02,381 --> 01:22:04,091
No, but...

1311
01:22:06,051 --> 01:22:09,138
But there is the dream
of someone else.

1312
01:22:36,165 --> 01:22:38,834
Grab a copy of
The Trumpet of the Swan.

1313
01:22:39,084 --> 01:22:40,627
This is a tragedy.

1314
01:22:42,379 --> 01:22:45,048
- These chairs for sale?
- Anything not nailed down.

1315
01:22:45,257 --> 01:22:46,633
Ten dollars, it's yours.

1316
01:22:46,884 --> 01:22:48,177
What are you going to do now?

1317
01:22:48,635 --> 01:22:52,264
I think I'll take some time.
I'm almost looking forward to it.

1318
01:22:52,556 --> 01:22:53,557
Good luck to you.

1319
01:22:55,976 --> 01:22:58,979
I came here every Saturday
when I was little.

1320
01:22:59,229 --> 01:23:02,357
I remember when your mother
gave me Anne of Green Gables.

1321
01:23:02,649 --> 01:23:06,487
"Read it with a box of Kleenex",
she told me.

1322
01:23:06,778 --> 01:23:09,114
- Could someone help me?
- She's looking down on you now.

1323
01:23:09,781 --> 01:23:11,033
I'm sure she is.

1324
01:23:11,325 --> 01:23:13,994
Why don't we bomb Fox Books?

1325
01:25:03,103 --> 01:25:03,937
Do you have...

1326
01:25:05,022 --> 01:25:06,982
...the "shoe" books?

1327
01:25:07,524 --> 01:25:08,525
Who's the author?

1328
01:25:08,775 --> 01:25:09,651
I don't know.

1329
01:25:10,027 --> 01:25:14,573
My friend said my daughter has to
read the "shoe" books, so here I am.

1330
01:25:14,823 --> 01:25:16,200
Noel Streatfeild.

1331
01:25:18,994 --> 01:25:21,830
Noel Streatfeild wrote
Ballet Shoes and Skating Shoes...

1332
01:25:22,122 --> 01:25:23,123
...and Theatre Shoes...

1333
01:25:23,415 --> 01:25:25,125
...and Dancing Shoes and...

1334
01:25:25,375 --> 01:25:28,128
I'd start with Ballet Shoes.
It's my favorite.

1335
01:25:29,171 --> 01:25:32,466
Although Skating Shoes
is completely wonderful.

1336
01:25:33,717 --> 01:25:35,093
But it's out of print.

1337
01:25:36,011 --> 01:25:39,306
Streatfeild.
How do you spell that?

1338
01:25:41,183 --> 01:25:45,896
S-T-R-E-A-T-F-E-I-L-D.

1339
01:25:47,523 --> 01:25:48,690
Thank you.

1340
01:25:49,608 --> 01:25:52,110
Kathleen Kelly. It was
like her name was in the air.

1341
01:25:52,861 --> 01:25:53,654
Just like that?

1342
01:25:53,904 --> 01:25:58,826
Everyone was talking about her today.
Kathleen Kelly and her "situation".

1343
01:25:59,117 --> 01:26:03,664
And I was thinking that she'd make
a great children's book editor.

1344
01:26:03,997 --> 01:26:05,457
What makes you think that?

1345
01:26:05,749 --> 01:26:06,542
Well...

1346
01:26:06,834 --> 01:26:08,377
...she knows everything.

1347
01:26:08,627 --> 01:26:11,547
She has flawless taste.
She's famous for it.

1348
01:26:11,797 --> 01:26:15,050
Salesmen swear by her.
If she likes the book, it sells.

1349
01:26:15,384 --> 01:26:16,176
Period!

1350
01:26:16,426 --> 01:26:18,220
You're going to offer her a job?

1351
01:26:18,428 --> 01:26:20,430
What else has she got to do?

1352
01:26:20,722 --> 01:26:23,475
- Now that she's destitute.
- Thanks to you.

1353
01:26:23,725 --> 01:26:26,395
- I don't see her working for you.
- Why not?

1354
01:26:26,645 --> 01:26:28,272
She lacks the killer instinct.

1355
01:26:28,522 --> 01:26:30,649
She's never fired anybody.
Look at that little shop.

1356
01:26:30,899 --> 01:26:33,110
Those people have been there forever.

1357
01:26:33,360 --> 01:26:35,362
Till recently, when they all
lost their jobs.

1358
01:26:35,612 --> 01:26:37,072
Thanks to you.

1359
01:26:37,823 --> 01:26:39,658
Hold the elevator!

1360
01:26:43,829 --> 01:26:45,289
Hello, Charlie.

1361
01:26:46,373 --> 01:26:50,669
I love how you've forgotten you had
any role in her current situation.

1362
01:26:50,919 --> 01:26:52,087
It's so obtuse.

1363
01:26:52,671 --> 01:26:54,548
It's so insensitive.

1364
01:26:54,840 --> 01:26:56,216
Reminds me of someone.

1365
01:26:56,508 --> 01:26:58,469
Who does it remind me of?

1366
01:27:00,095 --> 01:27:01,346
Me!

1367
01:27:06,351 --> 01:27:07,227
What is going on?

1368
01:27:08,061 --> 01:27:08,937
Could be stuck.

1369
01:27:16,695 --> 01:27:17,738
What are you doing?

1370
01:27:19,281 --> 01:27:20,908
I hope it doesn't plummet.

1371
01:27:22,117 --> 01:27:23,118
Can it do that?

1372
01:27:24,369 --> 01:27:26,747
It cannot plummet to the basement.

1373
01:27:27,873 --> 01:27:29,708
Hi, this is Joe Fox.
Who's this?

1374
01:27:30,125 --> 01:27:30,918
Juan?

1375
01:27:31,418 --> 01:27:35,214
We are stuck in the elevator
between the sixth and seventh floor.

1376
01:27:35,464 --> 01:27:36,465
There's four of us.

1377
01:27:36,757 --> 01:27:39,593
If you don't get
your ass up here now...

1378
01:27:39,843 --> 01:27:42,429
...and get us the hell out of here...!

1379
01:27:43,931 --> 01:27:44,890
Listen.

1380
01:27:45,182 --> 01:27:47,559
Call the super and then 9-1-1.

1381
01:27:48,519 --> 01:27:50,521
9-1-1.

1382
01:27:51,522 --> 01:27:53,398
The fire department, that's right.

1383
01:27:54,858 --> 01:27:56,568
Thank you very much.

1384
01:27:59,530 --> 01:28:00,447
We should jump in the air.

1385
01:28:01,073 --> 01:28:01,865
What?

1386
01:28:02,866 --> 01:28:03,784
We jump.

1387
01:28:04,034 --> 01:28:06,703
The elevator thinks no one
is here and it opens.

1388
01:28:11,375 --> 01:28:12,209
One...

1389
01:28:12,584 --> 01:28:13,377
...two...

1390
01:28:13,627 --> 01:28:15,170
...three. Jump!

1391
01:28:22,594 --> 01:28:23,846
If I get out of here...

1392
01:28:25,097 --> 01:28:27,599
...I'm going to start speaking
to my momma.

1393
01:28:28,976 --> 01:28:31,478
Wonder what she's doing
right this minute?

1394
01:28:32,271 --> 01:28:33,981
If I ever get out of here...

1395
01:28:34,815 --> 01:28:36,608
...I'm marrying Orit.

1396
01:28:37,317 --> 01:28:38,610
I love her.

1397
01:28:39,194 --> 01:28:42,406
I should marry her.
I don't know what's stopping me.

1398
01:28:49,413 --> 01:28:52,833
If I ever get out of here,
I'm having my eyes lasered.

1399
01:28:59,548 --> 01:29:01,008
If I ever get out...

1400
01:29:01,467 --> 01:29:03,677
Where are my Tic-Tacs?

1401
01:29:06,680 --> 01:29:07,931
What?

1402
01:29:10,893 --> 01:29:14,938
I came home tonight and
got into the elevator.

1403
01:29:15,147 --> 01:29:17,983
An hour later,
I got out of the elevator...

1404
01:29:18,233 --> 01:29:20,402
...and Brinkley and I moved out.

1405
01:29:20,694 --> 01:29:23,447
Suddenly everything had become clear.

1406
01:29:23,864 --> 01:29:25,324
It's a long story...

1407
01:29:25,741 --> 01:29:28,577
...full of the personal
details we avoid.

1408
01:29:28,827 --> 01:29:30,370
Let me just say...

1409
01:29:30,787 --> 01:29:33,373
...there was a man
in the elevator with me...

1410
01:29:33,790 --> 01:29:36,293
...who knew exactly what he wanted.

1411
01:29:36,919 --> 01:29:40,547
I found myself wishing
I were as lucky as he.

1412
01:29:44,051 --> 01:29:47,721
People are always telling you
that change is a good thing.

1413
01:29:47,971 --> 01:29:51,600
All they're saying is that something
you didn't want to happen...

1414
01:29:52,184 --> 01:29:53,644
...has happened.

1415
01:29:54,895 --> 01:29:57,231
My store is closing this week.

1416
01:29:57,606 --> 01:29:59,233
I own a store.

1417
01:29:59,441 --> 01:30:01,151
Did I ever tell you that?

1418
01:30:01,777 --> 01:30:03,570
It's a lovely store.

1419
01:30:03,821 --> 01:30:06,406
In a week it will be
something depressing...

1420
01:30:06,657 --> 01:30:08,283
...like a Baby Gap.

1421
01:30:11,161 --> 01:30:12,955
Soon we'll just be a memory.

1422
01:30:13,205 --> 01:30:17,751
Some foolish person will think
it's a tribute to this city.

1423
01:30:18,043 --> 01:30:22,714
The way it keeps changing on you,
or the way you can never count on it.

1424
01:30:23,215 --> 01:30:25,259
I know because that's
something I'd say.

1425
01:30:25,509 --> 01:30:27,302
But the truth is...

1426
01:30:28,595 --> 01:30:30,430
...I'm heartbroken.

1427
01:30:32,850 --> 01:30:35,435
I feel as if a part
of me has died...

1428
01:30:35,686 --> 01:30:38,605
...and my mother has died
all over again.

1429
01:30:41,066 --> 01:30:44,194
And no one can ever make it right.

1430
01:32:48,986 --> 01:32:50,237
What happened?

1431
01:32:52,739 --> 01:32:54,116
Oh, hell!

1432
01:32:56,076 --> 01:32:56,869
How are you?

1433
01:32:57,119 --> 01:33:00,330
Dad, you did pretty well.
At least you didn't marry her.

1434
01:33:00,581 --> 01:33:02,207
Welcome aboard.

1435
01:33:02,791 --> 01:33:04,418
It lasted a while.

1436
01:33:20,309 --> 01:33:21,852
You know...

1437
01:33:22,811 --> 01:33:24,813
...I've stayed on this boat after...

1438
01:33:25,731 --> 01:33:26,940
...let's see...

1439
01:33:27,316 --> 01:33:28,609
...your mother.

1440
01:33:30,027 --> 01:33:31,195
Laurette, the ballet dancer.

1441
01:33:31,904 --> 01:33:32,905
My nanny.

1442
01:33:33,822 --> 01:33:35,073
She was the nanny?

1443
01:33:37,659 --> 01:33:39,203
I forgot that.

1444
01:33:40,037 --> 01:33:41,205
How ironic!

1445
01:33:41,705 --> 01:33:43,707
Then there was the ice skater.

1446
01:33:43,999 --> 01:33:45,000
Also my nanny.

1447
01:33:45,250 --> 01:33:46,043
Really?

1448
01:33:47,920 --> 01:33:49,797
That's amazingly ironic!

1449
01:33:51,089 --> 01:33:52,174
And then there was Sybil.

1450
01:33:54,092 --> 01:33:54,968
It's an "A" word.

1451
01:33:55,260 --> 01:33:56,011
Astrologer.

1452
01:33:56,595 --> 01:33:57,971
Exactly.

1453
01:33:58,555 --> 01:34:01,558
Whose moon turned out to be
in someone else's house.

1454
01:34:02,434 --> 01:34:03,268
Just like Gillian.

1455
01:34:05,103 --> 01:34:06,104
Gillian ran off with someone?

1456
01:34:06,396 --> 01:34:07,356
The nanny.

1457
01:34:08,232 --> 01:34:09,107
Nanny Maureen?

1458
01:34:13,904 --> 01:34:16,240
Gillian ran off with Nanny Maureen.

1459
01:34:16,949 --> 01:34:18,242
You got it!

1460
01:34:18,617 --> 01:34:20,536
It's incredibly ironic.

1461
01:34:20,869 --> 01:34:21,662
That's true.

1462
01:34:21,912 --> 01:34:23,831
No other word for it.

1463
01:34:24,289 --> 01:34:26,208
Well, who's better than us?

1464
01:34:26,834 --> 01:34:28,877
Father and son, together at last.

1465
01:34:29,378 --> 01:34:31,171
Who did you say you broke up with?

1466
01:34:31,797 --> 01:34:33,340
Patricia.

1467
01:34:33,590 --> 01:34:34,383
You met her.

1468
01:34:35,259 --> 01:34:36,093
Would I like her?

1469
01:34:38,846 --> 01:34:40,389
Just kidding, son.

1470
01:34:41,723 --> 01:34:43,892
Now, is this beautiful or what?

1471
01:34:45,144 --> 01:34:48,730
I'll be living out of
a suitcase for at least 3 weeks.

1472
01:34:49,356 --> 01:34:52,734
And then there's
the inevitable legal hassle.

1473
01:34:53,026 --> 01:34:54,403
More of your inheritance
down the drain.

1474
01:34:54,945 --> 01:34:56,071
Don't worry about it.

1475
01:34:56,572 --> 01:34:57,406
I won't.

1476
01:34:57,781 --> 01:35:01,034
I just have to meet someone new.
That's the easy part.

1477
01:35:01,326 --> 01:35:02,286
Right, yeah.

1478
01:35:02,703 --> 01:35:06,582
A snap to find the one person in the
world who fills your heart with joy.

1479
01:35:06,874 --> 01:35:08,500
Don't be ridiculous.

1480
01:35:08,750 --> 01:35:11,503
Have I ever been with anybody
who fit that description?

1481
01:35:12,796 --> 01:35:14,047
Have you?

1482
01:35:41,325 --> 01:35:42,117
Who is it?

1483
01:35:44,286 --> 01:35:46,163
It's Joe Fox.

1484
01:35:48,040 --> 01:35:49,750
What are you doing here?

1485
01:35:52,252 --> 01:35:53,128
May I please come up?

1486
01:35:53,462 --> 01:35:54,463
No, I don't...

1487
01:35:54,755 --> 01:35:58,008
No, I don't really think that
that is a good idea, because...

1488
01:35:58,258 --> 01:35:59,510
...I have a...

1489
01:36:00,177 --> 01:36:01,637
...I have a terrible...

1490
01:36:01,929 --> 01:36:03,305
...cold.

1491
01:36:07,893 --> 01:36:08,811
Can you hear that?

1492
01:36:10,813 --> 01:36:13,941
I'm sniffling,
and I'm not really awake.

1493
01:36:14,233 --> 01:36:18,111
I'm taking echinacea and vitamin C
and sleeping practically...

1494
01:36:18,403 --> 01:36:20,781
...24 hours a day.
I have a temperature.

1495
01:36:21,448 --> 01:36:25,118
And I think I'm contagious.
So I would...

1496
01:36:25,410 --> 01:36:28,497
...I would really appreciate it
if you'd just go away.

1497
01:36:40,467 --> 01:36:42,094
Just a second!

1498
01:36:48,934 --> 01:36:51,478
Yes, just a second.

1499
01:37:04,283 --> 01:37:05,659
What are you doing here?

1500
01:37:06,118 --> 01:37:07,494
I heard you were sick.

1501
01:37:07,911 --> 01:37:09,454
And I was worried.

1502
01:37:11,123 --> 01:37:13,000
I wanted to make sure...

1503
01:37:15,711 --> 01:37:17,504
Is there somebody here?

1504
01:37:18,672 --> 01:37:20,466
It's the Home Shopping Network.

1505
01:37:20,757 --> 01:37:22,926
You buy any of those
porcelain dolls?

1506
01:37:23,218 --> 01:37:24,928
I was thinking about it.

1507
01:37:25,679 --> 01:37:28,182
- You put me out of business.
- Yes, I did.

1508
01:37:28,474 --> 01:37:30,017
Did you come to gloat?

1509
01:37:30,350 --> 01:37:32,186
- To offer me a job?
- I would never...

1510
01:37:32,478 --> 01:37:35,731
I have plenty of offers.
I got offered a job by...

1511
01:37:36,023 --> 01:37:38,066
-By my former...
- Your former?

1512
01:37:39,109 --> 01:37:39,943
We broke up.

1513
01:37:40,235 --> 01:37:43,572
That's too bad.
You were so perfect for each other.

1514
01:37:46,825 --> 01:37:49,661
I don't mean to say things like that.
No matter what you've done...

1515
01:37:49,953 --> 01:37:52,039
...there's no excuse
for my saying that.

1516
01:37:52,456 --> 01:37:53,624
Every time I see you...

1517
01:37:53,916 --> 01:37:56,001
Things like that just fly out.

1518
01:37:58,587 --> 01:37:59,755
I brought you flowers.

1519
01:38:03,717 --> 01:38:04,510
Thank you.

1520
01:38:09,973 --> 01:38:12,226
Why don't I put these in water?

1521
01:38:13,727 --> 01:38:15,562
You're sick.
You should sit down.

1522
01:38:21,485 --> 01:38:22,945
I need a vase.

1523
01:38:23,946 --> 01:38:25,864
Above the refrigerator.

1524
01:38:26,448 --> 01:38:28,784
There it is. Obviously.

1525
01:38:29,993 --> 01:38:32,746
George says hello, by the way.

1526
01:38:34,540 --> 01:38:35,999
He told me you were sick.

1527
01:38:36,333 --> 01:38:37,209
How is George?

1528
01:38:39,294 --> 01:38:41,130
Great, really great.

1529
01:38:41,547 --> 01:38:43,882
He's revolutionizing the place.

1530
01:38:44,133 --> 01:38:45,592
You can't work in his department...

1531
01:38:45,843 --> 01:38:48,428
...unless you have a Ph.D.
in Children's Literature.

1532
01:38:48,762 --> 01:38:50,305
I love daisies.

1533
01:38:51,598 --> 01:38:52,391
You told me.

1534
01:38:52,641 --> 01:38:54,017
They're so friendly.

1535
01:38:54,393 --> 01:38:57,646
Don't you think daisies
are the friendliest flower?

1536
01:38:57,938 --> 01:38:58,856
I do.

1537
01:39:00,107 --> 01:39:01,150
When did you break up?

1538
01:39:02,526 --> 01:39:03,652
Couple of weeks ago.

1539
01:39:04,528 --> 01:39:06,989
Everyone is breaking up.
You. Me.

1540
01:39:07,406 --> 01:39:10,909
This other person broke up
with someone in an elevator.

1541
01:39:11,160 --> 01:39:13,912
Or after it,
or just outside it, or...

1542
01:39:14,163 --> 01:39:15,873
It got stuck.

1543
01:39:16,456 --> 01:39:19,793
When I saw you at the coffee place,
I was waiting for him.

1544
01:39:20,085 --> 01:39:21,336
And I was...

1545
01:39:21,587 --> 01:39:22,838
Charming.

1546
01:39:23,464 --> 01:39:24,923
I was not charming.

1547
01:39:25,174 --> 01:39:26,884
You looked charming. Tea?

1548
01:39:28,802 --> 01:39:31,221
I was upset and horrible.

1549
01:39:32,139 --> 01:39:33,307
Honey?

1550
01:39:34,016 --> 01:39:35,267
I was the horrible one.

1551
01:39:35,517 --> 01:39:37,060
Well, that's true.

1552
01:39:37,352 --> 01:39:39,062
But I have no excuse.

1553
01:39:40,898 --> 01:39:42,608
I see what you're saying.

1554
01:39:43,609 --> 01:39:44,776
That's interesting.

1555
01:39:45,068 --> 01:39:47,029
I am a horrible person...

1556
01:39:47,279 --> 01:39:50,824
...therefore I have no choice
but to be horrible.

1557
01:39:51,116 --> 01:39:54,203
That's what you're saying.
But that's all right.

1558
01:39:54,411 --> 01:39:57,080
I put you out of business, so...

1559
01:39:57,706 --> 01:39:59,249
...you're entitled to hate me.

1560
01:40:00,709 --> 01:40:01,502
I don't hate you.

1561
01:40:03,212 --> 01:40:05,380
But you'll never forgive me.

1562
01:40:05,798 --> 01:40:06,590
Just like Elizabeth.

1563
01:40:07,132 --> 01:40:07,925
Who?

1564
01:40:08,634 --> 01:40:10,844
Elizabeth Bennet in
Pride and Prejudice.

1565
01:40:11,094 --> 01:40:12,095
She was too proud.

1566
01:40:12,346 --> 01:40:14,723
I thought you hated
Pride and Prejudice.

1567
01:40:15,015 --> 01:40:17,476
Or was she too prejudiced...

1568
01:40:17,768 --> 01:40:20,187
...and Mr. Darcy is too proud?

1569
01:40:20,479 --> 01:40:21,855
I can't remember.

1570
01:40:28,445 --> 01:40:29,988
It wasn't personal.

1571
01:40:30,322 --> 01:40:33,617
What is that supposed to mean?
I'm so sick of that!

1572
01:40:33,909 --> 01:40:37,204
All that means is that
it wasn't personal to you.

1573
01:40:37,496 --> 01:40:39,206
But it was personal to me.

1574
01:40:39,498 --> 01:40:41,708
It's personal to a lot of people.

1575
01:40:42,000 --> 01:40:45,003
What is so wrong
with being personal anyway?

1576
01:40:46,213 --> 01:40:47,506
Nothing.

1577
01:40:47,798 --> 01:40:51,927
Whatever else anything is,
it ought to begin by being personal.

1578
01:40:55,264 --> 01:40:57,641
My head is starting to get fuzzy.

1579
01:41:03,063 --> 01:41:04,940
Why did you stop by again?
I forget.

1580
01:41:06,525 --> 01:41:07,734
I wanted to be your friend.

1581
01:41:11,196 --> 01:41:13,282
I knew it wasn't possible.

1582
01:41:14,449 --> 01:41:17,452
Sometimes a guy
just wants the impossible.

1583
01:41:27,463 --> 01:41:29,298
Can I ask you a question?

1584
01:41:29,548 --> 01:41:30,716
What?

1585
01:41:32,551 --> 01:41:35,220
What happened with that guy
at the café?

1586
01:41:36,138 --> 01:41:37,222
Nothing.

1587
01:41:37,473 --> 01:41:39,266
But you're crazy about him?

1588
01:41:40,726 --> 01:41:41,643
Yes, I am.

1589
01:41:41,894 --> 01:41:45,272
Why don't you run off with him?
What are you waiting for?

1590
01:41:47,733 --> 01:41:49,651
I don't actually know him.

1591
01:41:50,486 --> 01:41:51,278
Really?

1592
01:41:53,906 --> 01:41:55,365
I only know him through...

1593
01:41:57,409 --> 01:41:58,785
You won't believe this.

1594
01:41:59,036 --> 01:42:00,662
Let me guess.

1595
01:42:01,246 --> 01:42:02,623
Through the Internet?

1596
01:42:08,837 --> 01:42:11,298
"You've got mail."

1597
01:42:15,302 --> 01:42:16,678
Those are powerful words.

1598
01:42:21,850 --> 01:42:22,893
I'm happy for him.

1599
01:42:25,020 --> 01:42:26,730
Although...

1600
01:42:28,065 --> 01:42:29,983
Could I just make a suggestion?

1601
01:42:30,651 --> 01:42:31,735
What?

1602
01:42:32,402 --> 01:42:33,487
I think...

1603
01:42:34,404 --> 01:42:36,114
...you should meet him.

1604
01:42:36,615 --> 01:42:38,242
Wait, I take that back.

1605
01:42:40,035 --> 01:42:42,871
Why would you meet
somebody you're crazy about?

1606
01:42:43,121 --> 01:42:46,792
I hardly think I need to take
advice from a person who...

1607
01:42:49,211 --> 01:42:51,338
I can see I bring out
the worst in you.

1608
01:42:51,630 --> 01:42:54,216
Let me help you
to not say something...

1609
01:42:54,508 --> 01:42:58,971
...you'll torture yourself about
for years to come.

1610
01:43:12,151 --> 01:43:14,319
I hope you feel better soon.

1611
01:43:15,696 --> 01:43:17,948
It'd be a shame to miss
New York in the spring.

1612
01:43:22,244 --> 01:43:23,787
Thank you for the daisies.

1613
01:43:36,049 --> 01:43:37,509
You take care.

1614
01:43:38,927 --> 01:43:39,928
I will.

1615
01:43:41,472 --> 01:43:42,264
Goodbye.

1616
01:44:02,576 --> 01:44:04,745
I've been thinking about this...

1617
01:44:04,995 --> 01:44:07,080
...and I think we should meet.

1618
01:44:13,796 --> 01:44:15,339
We should meet.

1619
01:44:16,048 --> 01:44:17,841
And we will meet.

1620
01:44:18,342 --> 01:44:22,304
But I'm in the middle
of a project that needs...

1621
01:44:27,559 --> 01:44:29,019
...tweaking.

1622
01:44:48,997 --> 01:44:50,457
Some tweaking?

1623
01:44:51,083 --> 01:44:52,960
A project that needed...

1624
01:44:53,293 --> 01:44:54,169
..."tweaking".

1625
01:44:54,461 --> 01:44:58,590
- That's what he said.
- T-W-E-A-K-I-N-G?

1626
01:44:59,299 --> 01:45:02,219
It sounds to me like he's married.

1627
01:45:02,678 --> 01:45:03,512
Three kids.

1628
01:45:03,804 --> 01:45:06,807
That's a terrible thing to say.
He couldn't be married.

1629
01:45:07,057 --> 01:45:08,225
How do you know?

1630
01:45:08,475 --> 01:45:10,644
Have you said, "Are you married?"

1631
01:45:10,936 --> 01:45:13,480
No, I'm not going to...

1632
01:45:14,940 --> 01:45:18,318
I know this is a little late
to be asking but...

1633
01:45:19,444 --> 01:45:20,904
...are you married?

1634
01:45:24,449 --> 01:45:25,909
Am I married?

1635
01:45:26,118 --> 01:45:28,036
What kind of question is that?

1636
01:45:28,662 --> 01:45:31,498
How can you ask me that?
Don 't you know me at all?

1637
01:45:31,832 --> 01:45:33,667
Wait, I get it.

1638
01:45:34,334 --> 01:45:37,588
Your friends are saying the reason
we haven't met is I'm married.

1639
01:45:37,838 --> 01:45:39,298
Am I right?

1640
01:45:40,632 --> 01:45:41,633
He didn't answer.

1641
01:45:41,925 --> 01:45:42,801
Yes, he did.

1642
01:45:43,093 --> 01:45:43,969
- He did not.
- He did!

1643
01:45:44,261 --> 01:45:45,971
He did. He nailed me.

1644
01:45:46,263 --> 01:45:50,142
He knew what I was after which is,
by the way, exactly like him.

1645
01:45:50,434 --> 01:45:53,645
He did not answer
the question, did he?

1646
01:45:57,524 --> 01:45:59,067
Maybe he's fat.

1647
01:46:00,402 --> 01:46:02,279
He's fat. He's a fatty.

1648
01:46:03,155 --> 01:46:04,031
I don't care.

1649
01:46:04,281 --> 01:46:06,200
You don't care that he's so fat...

1650
01:46:06,450 --> 01:46:10,120
...he has to be removed
from his house by a crane.

1651
01:46:10,370 --> 01:46:11,747
That is very unlikely.

1652
01:46:12,039 --> 01:46:14,333
That is completely ridiculous.

1653
01:46:15,042 --> 01:46:15,918
What's his handle?

1654
01:46:20,130 --> 01:46:21,590
I'm not going to write him.

1655
01:46:21,882 --> 01:46:23,884
You think I'm going to e-mail him?

1656
01:46:26,220 --> 01:46:27,346
All right, N-Y-1-5-2.

1657
01:46:28,096 --> 01:46:30,682
N-Y-1-5-2?

1658
01:46:30,974 --> 01:46:33,060
He's 152 years old.

1659
01:46:33,894 --> 01:46:36,563
He's had 152 moles removed...

1660
01:46:36,855 --> 01:46:39,775
...so now he's got
152 pockmarks on his...

1661
01:46:40,442 --> 01:46:42,069
...on his face.

1662
01:46:42,361 --> 01:46:45,781
The number of people who think
he looks like Clark Gable.

1663
01:46:46,114 --> 01:46:49,910
152 people who think
he looks like a Clark Bar.

1664
01:46:53,789 --> 01:46:57,334
- Why'd I even tell you?
- 152 stitches from his nose job.

1665
01:46:57,626 --> 01:47:00,629
The number of his souvenir
shot glasses...

1666
01:47:00,921 --> 01:47:04,383
...that he's collected
in his travels.

1667
01:47:05,384 --> 01:47:06,677
His address.

1668
01:47:07,177 --> 01:47:11,014
No, he would never do
anything that prosaic.

1669
01:47:15,102 --> 01:47:18,105
The only thing I
really care about is that...

1670
01:47:18,355 --> 01:47:21,483
...aside from the married thing
and the jail thing...

1671
01:47:21,733 --> 01:47:24,194
...is the boat thing.

1672
01:47:25,070 --> 01:47:25,863
What boat thing?

1673
01:47:26,155 --> 01:47:28,532
I could never be
with someone who had a boat.

1674
01:47:30,284 --> 01:47:31,535
I have a boat.

1675
01:47:34,288 --> 01:47:36,832
Which clinches it.
We'll never be together.

1676
01:47:37,207 --> 01:47:38,750
- How many?
- Three.

1677
01:47:39,042 --> 01:47:40,419
- Allow me.
- Thank you.

1678
01:47:40,627 --> 01:47:44,214
I could never be with
someone who likes Joni Mitchell.

1679
01:47:44,506 --> 01:47:47,509
"It's cloud's illusions I recall.
I really don't know clouds at all."

1680
01:47:47,759 --> 01:47:51,138
What does that mean?
Is she a pilot?

1681
01:47:51,388 --> 01:47:54,308
It must be a metaphor,
but I don't know what it is.

1682
01:47:55,976 --> 01:47:57,352
How's your book coming?

1683
01:47:57,603 --> 01:48:00,439
There's this children's
book editor I know.

1684
01:48:00,689 --> 01:48:03,358
She's excited to read it
when I'm finished.

1685
01:48:03,609 --> 01:48:05,861
Who would have thought
that I would write?

1686
01:48:06,153 --> 01:48:08,447
If I hadn't had all this time...

1687
01:48:09,406 --> 01:48:10,782
- You know what?
- What?

1688
01:48:11,366 --> 01:48:15,496
The truth is he was the one who
got me thinking about writing.

1689
01:48:16,622 --> 01:48:19,917
Mr. 152 Felony Indictments.

1690
01:48:21,001 --> 01:48:24,421
Mr. 152 Insights Into My Soul.

1691
01:48:27,132 --> 01:48:29,218
No competing with that.

1692
01:48:31,845 --> 01:48:34,014
I keep on bumping into you.

1693
01:48:35,808 --> 01:48:37,059
Hope your mango's ripe.

1694
01:48:38,101 --> 01:48:39,561
I think it is.

1695
01:48:40,187 --> 01:48:42,272
You want to bump into me...

1696
01:48:42,689 --> 01:48:44,900
...on Saturday around lunchtime?

1697
01:48:45,692 --> 01:48:46,944
Over there?

1698
01:48:53,408 --> 01:48:55,744
How about meeting...

1699
01:48:56,036 --> 01:48:57,913
...Saturday?

1700
01:48:58,872 --> 01:49:00,666
Four o 'clock.

1701
01:49:01,041 --> 01:49:03,001
There's a place
in Riverside Park...

1702
01:49:03,293 --> 01:49:06,213
...where the path curves
and there's a garden.

1703
01:49:06,463 --> 01:49:08,799
Brinkley and I will be waiting.

1704
01:49:09,508 --> 01:49:10,759
Okay, let's do it.

1705
01:49:11,051 --> 01:49:12,678
Ready? And...

1706
01:49:14,429 --> 01:49:15,722
There it goes!

1707
01:49:16,765 --> 01:49:18,058
Today?

1708
01:49:20,018 --> 01:49:22,354
I know. In Riverside Park.

1709
01:49:23,772 --> 01:49:25,732
That would mean he's a Westsider.

1710
01:49:25,983 --> 01:49:28,735
Maybe I've seen him
and don't even know it.

1711
01:49:28,944 --> 01:49:31,155
You could have seen him every day.

1712
01:49:31,405 --> 01:49:32,614
It's possible.

1713
01:49:32,906 --> 01:49:34,741
- He could be anyone.
- He could be that guy.

1714
01:49:35,033 --> 01:49:36,994
And those flowers are for you.

1715
01:49:38,662 --> 01:49:41,081
- Could be the Zipper Man.
- Who's that?

1716
01:49:41,373 --> 01:49:42,541
The Zipper Man.

1717
01:49:42,958 --> 01:49:43,792
Who is that?

1718
01:49:44,042 --> 01:49:46,503
He repairs zippers
on Amsterdam Avenue.

1719
01:49:46,753 --> 01:49:47,546
Will you cut it out?

1720
01:49:47,796 --> 01:49:50,132
You'd never have to buy new luggage.

1721
01:49:51,967 --> 01:49:55,804
The timing here is everything.
He's waited until you're primed.

1722
01:49:56,096 --> 01:49:56,930
See?

1723
01:49:57,222 --> 01:50:01,810
Until you are absolutely convinced
that there's no other man...

1724
01:50:02,102 --> 01:50:04,313
...that you could possibly love.

1725
01:50:06,648 --> 01:50:08,484
You know, sometimes I wonder.

1726
01:50:09,026 --> 01:50:10,068
What?

1727
01:50:13,530 --> 01:50:15,240
If I hadn't been Fox Books...

1728
01:50:15,449 --> 01:50:18,619
...and you hadn't been
The Shop Around The Corner...

1729
01:50:19,453 --> 01:50:21,747
...and you and I had just met.

1730
01:50:22,331 --> 01:50:23,582
I know.

1731
01:50:26,084 --> 01:50:28,086
I'd have asked for your number.

1732
01:50:29,880 --> 01:50:34,426
I wouldn't have been able to wait
24 hours before calling and saying:

1733
01:50:36,637 --> 01:50:38,889
"How about some coffee...

1734
01:50:39,139 --> 01:50:41,266
...or drinks or dinner...

1735
01:50:41,558 --> 01:50:43,018
...or a movie...

1736
01:50:44,144 --> 01:50:46,647
...for as long as
we both shall live."

1737
01:50:52,236 --> 01:50:54,780
You and I would never
have been at war.

1738
01:50:55,697 --> 01:51:00,202
The only thing we'd fight about
would be which video to rent.

1739
01:51:03,455 --> 01:51:04,832
Who fights about that?

1740
01:51:06,041 --> 01:51:07,584
Some people.

1741
01:51:09,128 --> 01:51:09,920
Not us.

1742
01:51:10,337 --> 01:51:12,131
We would never.

1743
01:51:16,760 --> 01:51:17,886
If only.

1744
01:51:23,350 --> 01:51:26,353
- I got to go.
- Let me ask you something.

1745
01:51:31,108 --> 01:51:34,820
How can you forgive this guy
for standing you up...

1746
01:51:35,070 --> 01:51:38,282
...and not forgive me
for this tiny little thing...

1747
01:51:39,700 --> 01:51:41,910
...of putting you out of business?

1748
01:51:47,332 --> 01:51:48,625
How I wish you would.

1749
01:51:51,503 --> 01:51:52,880
I really have to go.

1750
01:51:53,172 --> 01:51:54,882
You don't want to be late.

1751
01:54:09,766 --> 01:54:11,977
Don't cry, Shopgirl.

1752
01:54:13,645 --> 01:54:14,688
Don't cry.

1753
01:54:18,442 --> 01:54:19,985
I wanted it to be you.

1754
01:54:22,196 --> 01:54:24,531
I wanted it to be you so badly.
